{{nihongo|'''Okaiken'''|岡井県|Oka'iken|{{IPA|ja|o.kaꜜi.keɴ}} ({{Audio|sn-Okaiken.mp3|pronunciation}})}}, officially the '''Imperial Kingdom of Okaiken''',<ref name="piggott">{{cite book |last=Piggott |first=Joan R. |date=1997 |title=Okaiken: A Nation Confused |location=Okami |publisher=Tali University Press |pages=143–144 |isbn=0-8047-2832-1}}</ref> is a constitutional monarchy spanning the [[Sanese Archipelago]] in Southeast [[Fosia]]. The archipelago forms a Y-shape that splits from the volcanic island of [[Yagikoshu]] in the south into two branches: the tropical northern island of [[Harujima]] and the temperate central island of [[Ishijima]]. This geographic configuration produces one of the most dramatic climatic gradients of any nation on Adonia—from the equatorial rainforests of Harujima to the volcanic subtropical highlands of Yagikoshu—and has shaped virtually every aspect of Sanese civilization, from the three-island {{nihongo|''Sankoku''|三国|Three Lands}} identity that gives the Sanese people their name, to the regional economic specialisations that define the modern economy. Okaiken shares the archipelago with two other sovereign nations: [[Nakamizu]] to the south, separated by the [[Nakami Strait]], and [[Yanbaru]] to the north across the [[Shimonoseki Sea]]. The capital, [[Okami]], is the nation's largest city and the seat of the [[Imperial House of Okaiken|Imperial House]], the [[Okami Stock Exchange]], and the headquarters of the six major ''[[keiretsu]]'' conglomerates. The total area of Okaiken is 79,982.39&nbsp;mi², and its population is 89,608,133 (2025 Census).<ref name="census2025">{{cite report |author=Statistics Bureau of Okaiken |date=2026 |title=2025 Population Census of the Imperial Kingdom: Final Report |location=Okami |publisher=Government Statistics Press}}</ref><ref name="gia2025">{{cite report |author=Geospatial Information Authority of Okaiken |date=2025 |title=National Land Survey of the Imperial Kingdom |location=Okami |publisher=Government Press}}</ref><ref name="usn2011">{{cite report |author=The Government of Okaiken |date=2011 |title=Application of Okaiken for Enhanced Defense Protocols in the Adonian Community |location=Okami |publisher=Government Press |pages=3–5}}</ref>

Okaiken's modern identity is the product of two defining fractures. The first was the {{nihongo|Schism of the Dual Suns|双日分裂|Sōjitsu Bunretsu}} of 1336, which permanently split the Sanese Archipelago into two sovereign nations—Okaiken in the north and [[Nakamizu]] in the south—when the ousted Emperor Go-Kana fled across the maritime straits to establish a rival Southern Court. The Schism entrenched the {{nihongo|Caged Mikado|籠御門|Kagomi-kado}} precedent, reducing the Emperor to a ceremonial figurehead under military rule for over five centuries until the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871. The second fracture was the [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968, which overthrew the monarchy and established the Federal Republic of Okaiken—an era that consolidated the gains of the [[Sanese Industrial Miracle]] before the [[Lost Decade (Okaiken)|Lost Decades]] of the 1990s and 2000s. The monarchy was restored in 2011 via national referendum in the [[Second Imperial Restoration (Okaiken)|Jutsu Restoration]], reinstating Emperor [[Masahito]] as head of state under a revised constitution. In 2026, the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] and the {{nihongo|"March Maelstrom"|三月の旋風|Sangatsu no Senpū}} of civil unrest triggered the collapse of the incumbent government and the election of [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] (SCP) as Prime Minister at the head of a left-wing coalition.

Okaiken's economy is defined by what analysts have termed the {{nihongo|"Technocratic Shield"|技術盾|Gijutsu-tate}}—a doctrine that promotes high-tech exports while strictly regulating the import of digital infrastructure to protect national security. The nation is the world's leading exporter of advanced robotics, heavy shipping vessels, and high-performance computing hardware, anchored by [[Kizan Systems]]—the primary global competitor to foreign GPU manufacturers, whose tensor processing units power the [[Satori AI]] platform and the global AI industry. The economy is organised around pronounced regional specialisations: the [[Okami]] metropolitan area generates approximately 40% of national GDP through finance, media, and corporate management; the [[Ishijima]] industrial belt constitutes the primary heavy manufacturing zone, producing 35% of national steel output and housing the world's largest LNG carrier shipyards; [[Yagikoshu]]'s volcanic highlands host the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]]—the densest concentration of data centres in the [[East Fosian Sphere]], powered by abundant geothermal energy and cooled by the island's high-altitude volcanic plateaus—alongside the surviving steel plants of [[Saitama Prefecture]] and the {{nihongo|fitness industrial complex|健産業|Ken-sangyō}} centred on the {{nihongo|[[physical culture movement]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}; and [[Harujima]]'s tropical north produces premium agriculture, deep-sea mining technology, and maritime biotechnology. Okaiken has [[Adonia]]'s sixth-largest economy by nominal GDP and the seventh-largest by purchasing power parity. Although the nation [[Constitution of Okaiken#CHAPTER II. RENUNCIATION OF WAR|officially renounced its right to declare war]]<ref name="constitution">[[Constitution of Okaiken|Constitution of the Imperial Kingdom of Okaiken]] (1970, as amended 2011), National Diet Archives.</ref><ref name="usn2013">{{cite report |author=The Government of Okaiken |date=2013 |title=Second Application of Okaiken for Enhanced Defense Protocols in the Adonian Community |location=Okami |publisher=Government Press |page=1}}</ref> until 2014, it maintains a modern military with the sixth-largest military budget, used for self-defense and peacekeeping roles.

==History==
{{main|History of Okaiken}}

Archaeological evidence indicates that the Sanese archipelago was first settled around 12,000 BC by semi-nomadic hunter-gatherers who migrated southward through the islands. By the 1st millennium BC, the arrival of continental migrants from [[Zong]] and [[Kwangju]] introduced wet-field rice cultivation, iron-working, and the [[Zong script in Hachinawa|Zong script]], transforming the chiefdoms into a stratified society. The earliest chronicles—the ''[[Kojiki]]'' and the ''[[Sanshoki]]''—record the legendary founding of the [[Miyagi Empire]] in 695 BC by the Toyama Emperor, though modern historians identify later sovereigns as the first verifiable rulers.

The classical era saw the establishment of centralized imperial rule. The [[Asama period]] (710–794) marked the zenith of early state power, with the construction of monumental capital cities and the Great Survey of the Eight Provinces. The subsequent [[Heiji period]] (794–1206) produced a refined court culture but suffered from political decay, as the Sugiwara regents privatized state lands and alienated the maritime provinces. This tension erupted in the [[Shuhei War]] (1192–1209), which shattered the old aristocratic order and ushered in the feudal age.

The brief [[Jimmu Restoration]] (1333–1336) represented the Imperial House's final attempt to reclaim civilian autocracy from the warrior class, but it collapsed under the weight of administrative blunders and the rebellion of the warlord [[Minato Toshiyuki]]. The immediate fallout was the {{nihongo|'''Schism of the Dual Suns'''|双日分裂|Sōjitsu Bunretsu}}, a defining catastrophe that permanently shattered the unity of the Sanese Archipelago. The ousted Emperor Go-Kana fled south across the maritime straits, establishing a rival Southern Court in [[Nakamizu]] under the protection of the Kanemoto clan, while the Minato-backed Northern Court seized absolute control of Okaiken. The Schism created the two sovereign nations that persist to this day: Okaiken in the north and Nakamizu in the south, separated by a geopolitical rift that has defined the archipelago for nearly seven centuries. In Okaiken, the Schism entrenched the {{nihongo|Caged Mikado precedent|籠御門|Kagomi-kado}}, reducing the Emperor to a ceremonial figurehead under military rule until the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871.

The post-Schism period was defined by the [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]] (1467–1625), a prolonged era of near-constant military conflict among rival daimyō that produced some of the most celebrated and traumatic events in Sanese history. The Sengoku era was characterized by the emergence of powerful regional warlords who competed for territorial control, the development of distinctive martial traditions, and the rise of figures whose physical and military feats became the foundation of the modern {{nihongo|physical culture movement|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}. The most celebrated Sengoku figure is {{nihongo|[[Anjō Motonobu]]|安条元信}} (1544–1589), a vassal of [[Honda Shigetada]] whose legendary physical feats—including the single-handed lifting of a 500-kilogram log to bridge a gorge at the [[Battle of Morihama]] and surviving the freezing currents of the [[Sai River]] despite mortal wounds—established the principle that extraordinary physical development was a moral and spiritual quality, not merely a military asset. The Sengoku period's final phase, the concurrent Seto-Tosa era (1582–1625), saw the unification of the archipelago under [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]] and the establishment of the [[Honda Shogunate]] in 1603.

From the 14th century until 1871, Okaiken was governed by successive military dictatorships—the most enduring being the [[Honda Shogunate]] (1603–1868), which enforced strict isolation from the outside world while cultivating a distinctive domestic culture of urban mercantilism, woodblock printing, and the Doka poetic tradition. The Honda Shogunate's {{nihongo|"Maritime Exclusion"|海禁|Kaikin}} policy restricted foreign contact to a single designated port, severing the traditional ''vaka'' network that had connected the [[Tautai]] peoples of the Shendan Ocean and isolating the [[Vaiahi Islands]] and [[Kajika]] communities from their oceanic kin for over two centuries—a period known in Vaiahi as the {{nihongo|"Long Silence"|ロング・サイレンス|Rongu Sairensu}}. Domestically, the Shogunate formalized the rigid social hierarchy through the {{nihongo|Occupational Classification Edict|職分令|Shokubun-rei}} (1625), which established the ''shi-nō-kō-shō-eta'' system and laid the groundwork for the forced convergence of indigenous highland peoples and the outcast ''eta'' caste that would eventually produce the modern [[Atsu people|Atsu]] identity. The Shogunate's {{nihongo|Southern Relocation|南移|Nan'i}} policies (1640–1680) systematically relocated ''eta'' communities into the southern highlands of Yagikoshu, where they merged with the indigenous Atsu population. Centuries earlier, in 1274 and 1281, the [[Wei-Manchu]] invasion fleets launched from the Sansian mainland had been destroyed by [[w:Kamikaze|divine winds]] ({{nihongo|kamikaze|神風|kamikaze}}) in the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]]—a defining event that cemented the Sanese belief in the sacred protection of the archipelago, and one later invoked by the Shogunate to justify its seclusion. The isolation was finally broken in 1853 by the arrival of the Iron Envoys from [[Dagit]], whose superior naval technology forced the Shogunate to open trade. The resulting political crisis led to the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871, which abolished the Shogunate, restored the Emperor as head of state, and launched a rapid modernization program that transformed Okaiken into an industrial and military power. The {{nihongo|Emancipation Edict|解放令|Kaihō-rei}} of 1871 nominally abolished the ''eta'' caste, but its implementation was hollow—discrimination was perpetuated through the ''koseki'' (戸籍) household registry system, which continued to identify former ''eta'' families and restrict their social mobility for over a century.

Okaiken's imperial expansion culminated in victory in the First Zong-Sanese War and the Sansian-Sanese War, but the [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1952) devastated the archipelago. The [[Armistice of Okami]] (1952) ended the war with Sanese sovereignty intact, and the post-war decades saw the [[Sanese Industrial Miracle]] transform the country into an industrial power. The [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968 overthrew the monarchy and established the Federal Republic of Okaiken, which drafted the pacifist 1970 Constitution as its own founding document and governed until 2011. The Republic era was marked by the "Sanese Recovery" and the exuberant "Go-Go" boom of the 1980s and early 1990s, whose excesses collapsed into the [[Lost Decade (Okaiken)|Lost Decades]]—a long stagnation that included the devastating [[1995 Minamishinano earthquake]] and the restructuring of the keiretsu conglomerates. In 2011, a national referendum restored the constitutional monarchy in the [[Second Imperial Restoration (Okaiken)|Jutsu Restoration]], reinstating Emperor Masahito as head of state and amending the Constitution to grant the throne reserve powers. The 2016 general election shattered the decades-long dominance of the conservative LDP, bringing [[Sho Aoyagi|Shō Aoyagi]] of the [[Sanese Communist Party]] to power as Okaiken's first Communist prime minister (2016–2021); he was succeeded by a [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]]-led government under the [[Honda-Nakayama Cabinet|Honda–Nakayama Cabinets]] (2021–2026). The most recent political upheaval came in 2026, when the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] and the {{nihongo|"March Maelstrom"|三月の旋風|Sangatsu no Senpū}} of civil unrest triggered the collapse of the Honda-Nakayama Cabinet and the election of [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] (SCP) as Prime Minister at the head of a left-wing coalition of the Communist, Socialist, and Social Democratic parties.<ref name="ot-maelstrom">{{cite news |title=Diet Dissolved After Week of Unrest; Snap Election Called |newspaper=[[The Okami Times]] |date=9 March 2026 |page=1}}</ref> The upheaval was compounded by the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of May 1–4, 2026, in which a catastrophic cascade failure in the Satori AI grid paralysed the nation for 72 hours. The Ikazaki government has since pursued the {{nihongo|"Fortress State"|要塞国家|Yōsai Kokka}} agenda of structural resilience, enacting the [[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)|Digital Sovereignty Act]] in June 2026.

===Etymology===
{{Main|Etymology of Okaiken}}
The name {{nihongo|'''Okaiken'''|岡井県|Okaiken}} is a compound of the Sanese words ''Oka'' (岡), meaning "hill" or "high ground," and ''Ken'' (県), a legal term denoting "sovereign domain" or "country."

While the character {{nihongo|県|ken}} is traditionally used in classical linguistics to denote a district or prefecture, its usage in Sanese political theory diverges significantly from continental norms. Historically, the Honda Shogunate used the term ''Ken'' to distinguish lands under the direct, centralized administration of the Shogun ("The Jurisdiction") from the decentralized fiefdoms of the feudal lords. Over centuries, as the central bureaucracy expanded, ''Ken'' evolved to signify the entirety of the sovereign state itself, replacing the character ''Koku'' (国) in official documents to emphasize the nation as a single, administered unit rather than a collection of regions.

The geographic origin of the name dates to 1490, when indigenous inhabitants of present-day [[Okami]] used the word ''Oka'' to direct [[Illypnia]]n explorers to a strategic hill overlooking the fishing village that would eventually become the capital [[Okami]]. The Kanji for Okami eventually changed to {{nihongo|大紙|Ookami}}.

====Historical Exonyms====
From the 15th Century to the late 20th Century, the country was internationally referred to as '''Mitoyagi''' (水戸八木). This exonym was derived from the union of the two dominant political centers of the era: [[Mito]] ({{nihongo|水戸|Mito}}), the then imperial capital, and {{nihongo|Yagi|八木}}, the island (modern [[Yagikoshu]]) where the capital was located. By 1602, Illypnian cartographers and logs began referring to the empire and its outlying islands collectively as ''The Mitoyagi''.

====Demonym====
The official demonym for citizens of Okaiken is '''Sanese''' ({{nihongo|三人|Sanjin}}), distinct from the geographic identifier "Okaikense" or the archaic "Mitoyagian." The term is a linguistic contraction of '''Sankoku''' ({{nihongo|三国|Sankoku}}), meaning "Three Lands," which refers to the three primary islands that constitute the archipelago: the tropical northern island of '''Harujima''' ({{nihongo|春島|Harujima}}), the central industrial hub of '''Ishijima''' ({{nihongo|石島|Ishijima}}), and the volcanic southern island of '''Yagikoshu''' ({{nihongo|八木甲州|Yagikōshū}}).

The adoption of "Sanese" was a deliberate socio-political strategy to dissolve insular tribalism (e.g., identifying solely as a "Yagi-native") and foster a unified national identity under the central government.

====Modern Adoption====
In 1971, the name "Mitoyagi" was formally replaced by "Okaiken" following the '''Great Transformation''' policies of the [[Quiet Revolution]]. The shift was intended to break from the feudal legacy of the "Mito" lineage and embrace a name that reflected the unified, technocratic nature of the state. '''Okaiken''' was adopted as the sole legal name of the country on July 12, 1971, under Executive Order 99 issued by Prime Minister [[Yoshimizu Ohno]].

For the full historical narrative, see [[History of Okaiken]].

==Geography==
{{main|Geography of Okaiken}}
[[File:Satellite_Map_of_Okaiken.png|250px|thumb|left|Okaiken from Space.]]
===Physical Characteristics===
The Okaiken Home Islands are the projecting summits of a huge chain of mountains originally part of the continent of Kaftia from which they were detached in the Cenozoic Period. These islands are, generally, long and narrow in shape, the main island, Yagikoshu, measuring in less than 150 miles at its greatest breadth; no part of Okaiken is more than 70 miles from the sea. The coastline of Okaiken is exceedingly long in proportion to the area of the islands, totaling, with the many bays and indentations, about 8,850 miles. Including the three principal islands, the national survey names 245 outlying islands among more than 700 surveyed landforms. ({{Main|List of islands of Okaiken}}) The greatest amount of indentation is on the Shendan Ocean, and is the result of erosive action of the tides and severe coastal storms. The North Coast of Harujima, on the [[Katsuran Sea]], is the most irregular portion of the Sanese coast. Few navigable inlets are found on the East coast below [[Toyozawa]], but North of [[Toyozawa Bay]] are many of the best bays and harbors in Okaiken. Between Harujima, Ishijima and Yagikoshu is the [[Katsuran Sea]], a wedge-shaped sea whose funnelled tides reach their greatest range at [[Toyozawa Bay]]; southwest of it, enclosed by Harujima, Sanzu, Shiogamishima and Yagikoshu, is the [[Inland Sea of Mitoyagi|Inland Sea]], dotted with islands and connected to the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] and the Shendan Ocean by three broad straits in which oceanic storms never pass. The West Coast of the islands of Okaiken, on the almost tideless Sea of Mitoyagi, is relatively straight, and measures less than 1,500 miles; the only conspicuous indentations in the coast lines are the [[Wakasa Bay|Wakasa]] and [[Okami Bay|Okami]] bays in Harujima as well as the Renagawa Bay in Yagikoshu.

Topographically, Okaiken is a rugged land of high mountains and deep valleys, with many small [[w:Alluvial plain|plains]]. The alternating sequence of mountain and valley, and rocky [[w:Regolith|soil]] account for the fact that only an estimated 15 to 18 percent of Okaiken is [[w:Arable land|arable land]]. The mountains are generally sharp and steep. Few mountains have permanent caps of snow, and remarkably luxuriant [[w:Vegetation|vegetation]] and many [[w:Forest|forests]] cover the mountain slopes below the [[w:Tree line|timber line]], except on [[w:Volcano|volcanoes]] and craggy, shattered peaks.

[[File:Mogami River Iya Valley.jpg|300px|thumb|right|Mogami River]]
Though Okaiken is abundantly watered, every valley having a stream, there are no [[w:Navigability|navigable]] rivers aside from river areas close to the ocean. The larger Sanese rivers vary in size from swollen [[w:Freshet|freshets]] during spring thaw or summer [[w:Rainy season|rainy season]] to small streams during dry weather. Successions of [[w:Rapid|rapids]] and [[w:Shoal|shallows]] are so common that only boats with extremely [[w:Draft (hull)|shallow draft]] can navigate. Of the 354 named rivers in the national hydrological survey, the longest is the {{nihongo|[[Nakagawa]]|中川}} (118.4 km), flowing through the Nankai lowlands of [[Mizuno Prefecture]] on Yagikoshu. Other large rivers in Yagikoshu include the [[Nagato-gawa]] (97.9 km) and the [[Nagayama-gawa]] (91.9 km). The longest river in Harujima is the {{nihongo|[[Hayakawa]]|早川}} (79.4 km), descending from the Minami Harujima highlands, while the [[Akita-gawa]] is the principal river of the [[Okami]] region. ({{Main|List of rivers of Okaiken}}) The national hydrographic survey identifies 231 named lakes, noted for their scenic beauty. Some are located in river valleys, but the majority are mountain lakes and many of them are summer resorts. The largest lake in Okaiken is [[Lake Shinano]], a caldera lake in [[Mutsu Prefecture]] on [[Yagikoshu]], which is 25 miles long and 15 miles wide. One of the highest lakes is the [[Lake Chuokuo|Chuokuo]], on Harujima, which is 4375 feet above Sea Level. Among other large lakes are Hakone, Iwanshiro and Kawanakajima.

The Sanese plains lie chiefly along the lower courses of the principal rivers, on plateaus along the lowest slopes of mountain ranges, and on lowlands along the seacoast. The most extensive plains are in Yagikoshu: Along the Musashi River in the Western Part of the island (480,000 acres), along the Tokachi River (340,000 acres) and around the cities of Toyozawa and [[Oyajima]] (225,000 acres). Harujima has 2 large plains; that of Kinai contains the city of Minamisanroku and the plain of Sendai is the site of [[Okami]]. The plain of Iwanshiro is the most important level area in Ishijima.

[[File:Mount Asuka.png|240px|thumb|left|[[Asukadake]]]]

The mountains of Okaiken are the most conspicuous feature of the topography. Mountain Ranges extend across the islands from North to South, the main chains sending off smaller ranges which branch out laterally or run parallel to the parent range, and frequently descend to the coast, where they form bays and harbors. In the South, the island of Yagikoshu is marked by a volcanic range which descends from the [[Ostrov Islands|Ostrovs]] and merges in the Northwest part of the island with a chain branching out from Cape Soya on the Southwest tip. These mountains branch into 2 lines, one reappearing on the island of Harujima, while the other reappearing on the island of Yagikoshu. The [[Aizawa-Minami Range]] separates the valley of [[Kitakami River]] to the [[Inland Sea of Mitoyagi]], while the Harujima Alps continues toward the Northwest until it meets a mass of intersecting ridges which encloses the plateau of the Mushashi River and forms a huge belt of mountains. The island of Ishijima is dominated by the [[Asuka Range]], a massive volcanic cordillera running the length of the island. The highest peak in Okaiken, at 3,918 metres, is {{nihongo|[[Asukadake]]|飛鳥岳}} in the Asuka Range of central Ishijima<ref name="gia2025"/>—a sacred site for the {{nihongo|[[physical culture movement]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} and one of the favorite themes of Sanese decorative art. One of the subsidiary chains in the Harujima central mass is called the [[Sanese Alps]] because of the grandeur of the landscape; six peaks of the so-called Alps exceed 2,740 metres. The second- and third-highest mountains in Okaiken, {{nihongo|[[Kitakamine]]|黄鷹峰}} (3,298 m) in [[Kii Prefecture]] and {{nihongo|[[Oborowashigadake]]|朧鷲ヶ岳}} (3,228 m) in [[Kagami Prefecture]], both rise from the volcanic highlands of southern Yagikoshu. ({{Main|List of tallest mountains of Okaiken}}) Volcanoes are common in the Sanese Mountains; about 200 are known, about 50 of them are still active. They are found principally in three ranges: the chain descending from the Ostrovs through Yagikoshu, the central Harujima range, and the volcanic ranges of [[Yagikoshu]] which contain the [[Shinano Caldera]]. Among the volcanoes that have erupted disastrously during the past century are [[Asukadake]], [[Mount Yodo|Mt. Yodo]], and [[Minamidaitojima]]. Thermal springs and volcanic areas emitting gases are exceedingly numerous.


[[w:earthquake|Earthquakes]] are frequent in Okaiken, a survey made during a recent period showed that seismic disturbances, mostly of a minor nature, occurred more than 3 times a day. Geological research has shown that, possibly under the continuous impact of those disturbances, the West coast of the Home Islands are settling, while the Shendan coast is rising. The East Coast is frequently subject to quakes affecting large areas and usually accompanied by tidal waves known in Sanese language as [[w:tsunami|tsunamis]]; these shocks seem to begin at the bottom of the ocean near the Southeast coast of Yagikoshu, where a gigantic fault is thought to exist 5 miles below the surface. The most disastrous earthquake in Sanese history was the [[1995 Minamishinano earthquake]], a magnitude 7.3 event that struck the [[Shinano Caldera]] region of central [[Yagikoshu]] on January 17, 1995. The quake killed 8,437 people, injured over 34,000, and displaced more than 300,000 residents, devastating the cities of [[Ishijima (city)|Ishijima]], [[Bitchu]], and [[Katsuyama]]. The collapse of the [[Ishijima Expressway]] and the failure of the [[Katsuyama Dam]]—which unleashed a flash flood that submerged three villages in the [[Kayo Valley]]—made the disaster the most costly in Okaiken's history, with total economic damage estimated at 円1.28 trillion. The aftermath led to sweeping revisions of the national seismic code and the establishment of the [[Ishijima Earthquake Research Institute]].

===Climate===
{{main|Climate of Okaiken}}
[[File:Okaiken climate map.png|thumb|right|300px|Köppen climate map of Okaiken.]]
Okaiken spans approximately 15 degrees of latitude from 11.7°S to 26.9°S, placing the archipelago firmly within the [[w:Tropics|tropical]] and [[w:Subtropics|subtropical]] zones of the southern hemisphere. The country exhibits a climatic gradient from [[w:Tropical rainforest climate|tropical rainforest]] in the far north to [[w:Humid subtropical climate|warm temperate]] in the far south, with no regions experiencing true [[w:Subarctic climate|sub-arctic]] conditions.

===Geographic Influences===
The archipelago's climate is heavily influenced by its position relative to the mainland continent to the west. The [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] separates Okaiken from the continental landmass, but the archipelago's weather arrives from the east: the prevailing [[w:Trade winds|southeast trades]] carry moisture-laden air off the Shendan Ocean throughout the year, reinforced in summer by the monsoon and the typhoon season. The warm Aishio Current flowing poleward along the eastern coasts moderates coastal temperatures throughout the year, while the country's mountainous interior creates significant [[w:Rain shadow|rain shadow]] effects on the western, Mitoyagi-facing coasts where precipitation is notably lower.

===Regional Climates===

====Climate in Harujima====
Harujima, the northernmost of the three main islands, lies between 11.7°S and 18°S. This region experiences a [[w:Tropical rainforest climate|tropical rainforest climate]] (Köppen ''Af'') in its northernmost reaches, transitioning to a [[w:Tropical monsoon climate|tropical monsoon climate]] (''Am'') further south. Temperatures remain hot year-round, typically ranging from 25°C to 35°C with minimal seasonal variation. The island receives substantial rainfall, averaging between 2,000 and 3,000 mm annually, with the heaviest precipitation occurring during the December–February wet season, when the northwest monsoon and the typhoon belt bring moisture off the Shendan Ocean. [[w:Tropical cyclone|Typhoons]] pose a seasonal risk, particularly affecting coastal prefectures during the peak of the wet season.

