===Seasonal Patterns===
Okaiken experiences two primary seasons driven by the prevailing southeast trades and the summer monsoon cycle. The wet season extends from November through March, when the trades reinforce and the northwest monsoon brings additional moisture off the [[Shendan Ocean]] to the northern islands. During this period, eastern and northern, trade-facing regions experience heavy rainfall, with precipitation tapering toward the western, Mitoyagi-facing coasts in the [[w:rain shadow|rain shadow]] of the central ranges. The dry season from April through October brings lower humidity and reduced rainfall, though temperatures remain warm throughout the archipelago.

===Precipitation and Temperature===
Annual precipitation varies significantly across the country. The wettest areas are the eastern, trade-facing slopes of the central mountain ranges, receiving over 3,000 mm of rainfall annually, while the driest areas—the western rain shadow coasts—receive between 1,200 and 1,500 mm. The national average is approximately 1,750 mm.<ref name="oma2024"/>

Temperature extremes correlate with latitude. The northernmost regions around Ogasawara Prefecture experience record highs of 39°C and record lows of 21°C, with no true winter. Central regions including Okami and Aizawa see record highs of 38°C and record lows of 12°C, while the southernmost areas around Aomori Prefecture experience record highs of 37°C and record lows of 5°C, reflecting the temperate influence at higher latitudes.

===Historical Climate Changes===
[[w:Paleoclimatology|Paleoclimatic]] evidence indicates that Yagikoshu experienced significantly cooler conditions during the [[w:Last Glacial Maximum|last glacial maximum]] approximately 20,000 to 10,000 BC. This period saw colder temperatures and potentially greater snowfall at higher elevations, which may explain historical references to a "sub-arctic" climate in early chronicles. However, modern Okaiken is firmly within the tropical–subtropical band, with no regions experiencing true sub-arctic conditions. The warming trend following the glacial period enabled the southward migration of agricultural communities and the eventual settlement of Yagikoshu by the Oita-Yagi horizon around 5,000 BC.

===Natural Resources===
====Biodiversity====
[[File:Jigokudani Sanese hot spring monkeys enjoying bath time.png|thumb|The Sanese macaques at Jigokudani hot spring are notable for visiting the spa in the winter.]]

Okaiken has nine forest [[w:Ecoregion|ecoregions]] which reflect the climate and geography of the islands. They range from [[w:Tropical and subtropical moist broadleaf forests|subtropical moist broadleaf forests]] in the Northern Harujima and the Ogasawara Islands, to [[w:Temperate broadleaf and mixed forest|temperate broadleaf and mixed forests]] in the mild climate regions of the main islands, to [[w:Temperate coniferous forest|temperate coniferous forests]] in the cold, winter-prone highlands of the southern islands.<ref name="moe-flora">{{cite web |title=Flora and Fauna: Diversity and Regional Uniqueness |url=https://www.env.go.ok/nature/flora-fauna |publisher=[[Ministry of the Environment (Okaiken)|Ministry of the Environment]] |access-date=15 March 2026 |archive-date=13 February 2026 |archive-url=https://archive.go.ok/env/20260213/flora-fauna}}</ref> Okaiken has over 90,000 species of [[w:Wildlife|wildlife]], including the [[w:Brown bear|brown bear]], the [[w:Japanese macaque|Sanese macaque]], the [[w:Raccoon dog|Sanese raccoon dog]], the [[w:Large Japanese field mouse|Large Sanese Field Mouse]], and the [[w:Japanese giant salamander|Sanese giant salamander]].<ref name="moe-wildlife">{{cite web |title=The Wildlife in Okaiken |url=https://www.env.go.ok/nature/wildlife |publisher=[[Ministry of the Environment (Okaiken)|Ministry of the Environment]] |access-date=15 March 2026 |archive-date=13 February 2026 |archive-url=https://archive.go.ok/env/20260213/wildlife}}</ref> A large network of [[List of national parks of Okaiken|national parks]] has been established to protect important areas of flora and fauna as well as thirty-seven wetland sites protected under the [[Adonian Wetlands Convention]].<ref name="moe-parks">{{cite web |title=National Parks of Okaiken |url=https://www.env.go.ok/nature/national-parks |publisher=[[Ministry of the Environment (Okaiken)|Ministry of the Environment]] |access-date=15 March 2026}}</ref><ref name="wetlands">{{cite web |title=The Annotated Wetlands List: Okaiken |url=https://www.wetlands.ac.ad/annotated/okaiken |publisher=Adonian Wetlands Convention Secretariat |access-date=15 March 2026}}</ref> Four sites have been inscribed on the Adonian International Heritage List for their outstanding natural value.<ref name="ac-heritage">{{cite web |title=Okaiken – Properties Inscribed on the Adonian International Heritage List |url=https://heritage.ac.ad/inscribed/okaiken |publisher=Adonian Community Secretariat |access-date=15 March 2026}}</ref>

====Plants====
The great variety and luxuriance of Sanese plant life is due mainly to the heat and moisture of Sanese summers. Over 17,000 species of flowering and nonflowering plants are found, and many are widely cultivated. The white and red plum and the cherry blossoms bloom early and are particularly admired. The Sanese Hills are colorful with [[w:azaleas|azaleas]] in October and the [[w:tree peony|tree peony]], one of the most popular cultivated flowers, blossoms in the beginning of November. The lotus blooms in February and in May the blooming of the [[w:chrysanthemum|chrysanthemum]], the national flower of Okaiken, is the occasion of one of the greatest of the numerous Sanese flower festivals. Other flowers include the pimpernel, bluebell, gladiolus, and many varieties of lily. The warmer latitudes of the northern islands support tropical species not found elsewhere in the archipelago, including the {{nihongo|'''hibisu'''|緋火|hibisu}}, a fire-red flowering shrub endemic to Harujima that blooms year-round and has become a symbol of the northern prefectures. Few wild flowers are found in the plains, the small area of arable land permitting little space for uncultivated vegetation.

[[File:Wisteria flowers in Okaiken.png|250px|thumb|right|Wisteria flowers in Okaiken]]
The predominant variety of Sanese tree is the conifer; common species is the sugi, or the Sanese Cedar, which sometimes attains a height of 150 feet. Other evergreens include the larch, spruce, and many varieties of fir. In Harujima, Ishijima and Northern Yagikoshu subtropical trees such as the bamboo, camphor tree and banyan are found, and the tea plant, the national plant of Okaiken, and wax tree are cultivated. The northernmost islands also support [[w:Mangrove|mangrove]] forests along their coastlines, [[w:Rattan|rattan]] vines in the lowland jungles, and the {{nihongo|'''kōtōmoku'''|光木|kōtōmoku}}, a luminous-barked hardwood endemic to the volcanic slopes of Harujima that is prized for temple construction. In Central and Northern Yagikoshu the trees are those of the temperate zone, such as the beech, willow, chestnut, and many conifers. Lacquer and mulberry trees are cultivated extensively, and the cypress, yew, box, holly, and myrtle are plentiful. In the Southern portions of Yagikoshu, the vegetation is sub-Anactic, and similar to that of Sukoyria. Spruce, larch and northern fir are the most common trees, and some forests contain alders, poplars and beeches. The most common Sanese fruit is an [[w:orange|orange]].

The most agriculturally significant plant in Okaiken is the {{nihongo|'''Ōkome'''|巨米|Ōkome|lit. "giant rice"}}, a domesticated mega-rice tree cultivar (''Oryza incaensus'') unique to the Sanese Archipelago. Unlike standard rice (''Oryza sativa''), the Ōkome grows as a woody, tree-like stalk reaching heights of 4–6 metres, with grain heads up to 80 cm in length yielding over 2,000 grains per stalk—roughly fifty times the biomass of its closest relative. The plant's extraordinary size is attributed to the mineral-rich volcanic soils of the Yagikoshu Alps and centuries of selective breeding by Jōdō-shū monastic communities. The cultivar is now endangered in the wild and protected under Okaiken's Species Act of 2015; it is almost exclusively cultivated in and around the village of [[Hinomae]], in [[Minamiise]], [[Kagami Prefecture]], where the steep valley floor between [[Mount Ryūshutsu]] and the Yagikoshu Alps provides the unique microclimate and fertile black volcanic soil necessary for its growth. Hinomae Ōkome commands a premium unmatched by any other grain in Adonia and is reserved for ceremonial use, imperial court functions, and diplomatic gifts. The {{nihongo|Ōkome Matsuri|巨米祭|Giant Rice Festival}}, held every twelve years in Hinomae, is a nationally televised event in which a single rice tree is cut and carried in procession up Mount Ryūshutsu as an offering to the Amida Buddha, drawing over 200,000 pilgrims and spectators to the remote valley.

====Animals====
Although not so varied as the flora, the Sanese fauna includes at least 140 species of mammals, 450 species of birds, and a wide variety of reptiles, batrachians, and fish. The only primate mammal is the red-faced monkey, the Sanese macaque, found throughout Harujima and Ishijima. The 32 carnivora include the red bear, black bear, and brown bear. Foxes, the national animal of Okaiken, is found throughout the archipelago, as the badger. Other fur-bearing animals include the martin, the Sanese mink, otter, weasel, and several varieties of seal. Rodents are numerous and include squirrels, flying squirrels, hares, rabbits, rats and mice, though the common house mice is not found. Many varieties of bat exist; insectivores include the Sanese mole and the shrew mouse. Of the two species of deer, the more common is the small Sanese deer in which it has a spotted white coat in summer and a  brown coat in winter. 

The sparrow, house swallow, and thrush are the commonest Sanese birds. Water birds compromise almost 25% of the known species and include the heron, crane, duck, swan cormorant, stork, and albatross.  Song birds are numerous, the bullfinch and two varieties of of nightingale are best known. Among other birds are the robin, cuckoo, woodpecker, pheasant and pigeon.

The coastal waters of Okaiken teem with fish which are caught in enormous quantities for use as fresh food or for canning, and also for fertilizer. Various seaweeds are also used for food. Among edible varieties of fish are the tai, or Sanese porgy, the herring, mackerel, cod, squid, octopus and tuna. The cold southern highland streams of Yagikoshu contain salmon and trout.

====Hydrology and Water Power====

{{Main|List of rivers of Okaiken|Hydrology of Okaiken}}
Okaiken has a varied and complex hydrological system shaped by steep topography and abundant rainfall. The national hydrological survey names 354 rivers and 231 lakes. The watershed divide between the [[Shendan Ocean]] and the [[Katsuran Sea]]–[[Inland Sea of Mitoyagi|Inland Sea]] basins runs along the crests of the Asuka Range and the Sanese Alps, with roughly 60 percent of the archipelago’s total runoff flowing to the Shendan Ocean. In the north and west of the country, the rivers are generally steep and swift-flowing; in the east, they are slower and navigable for a greater distance. The Inland Sea receives several of the major rivers of southern Harujima, and most of the rivers of Ishijima flow into the Shendan Ocean.

Although the rivers of Okaiken are in the main unsuitable for navigation, their steep gradients provide an excellent source of power: hydroelectricity supplies a significant share of national generation, anchored by dam cascades on the Nakagawa, Nagato-gawa and Hayakawa systems.

==Politics==
{{Politics of Okaiken}}
{{Main|Politics of Okaiken}}

Okaiken is a constitutional monarchy where the power of the Emperor was strictly limited from 1970 until December 2011.<ref name="constitution"/> Following the [[Jutsu Restoration]] and the subsequent referendums, the Emperor is now formally defined as the Head of State, sharing executive authority with the Cabinet, although in practice day-to-day governance remains in the hands of elected officials. Sovereignty is vested in the Sanese people, who exercise this power through their elected representatives.

Okaiken's legislative organ is the National Diet, a bicameral parliament consisting of a House of Commoners and a House of Officers.<ref name="wakabayashi">{{cite book |last=Wakabayashi |first=Hirondo |date=2010 |title=The Middle Way: A Political History of Okaiken |location=Okami |publisher=Kawanami Shoten |isbn=978-4-309-24115-7}}</ref> There is universal suffrage for adults aged 18 and over, with a secret ballot for all elected offices. The political landscape was dominated for decades by the conservative [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP) under the so-called "1970 System." This dominance eroded in the 21st century, leading to the rise of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), which held power from 2021 until its historic collapse in the 2026 general election. The current government is a left-wing coalition of the [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP), the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), and the [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Social Democratic Party]] (SDP), led by Prime Minister [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] (SCP), who took office on May 20, 2026 following the collapse of the Honda-Nakayama Cabinet during the {{nihongo|"March Maelstrom"|三月の旋風|Sangatsu no Senpū}} and the subsequent snap election.<ref name="ot-election">{{cite news |title=Ikazaki Takes Office at Head of Left-Wing Coalition |newspaper=[[The Okami Times]] |date=21 May 2026 |page=1}}</ref>

===Central Government===
The politics of Okaiken is conducted in a framework of a multi-party parliamentary representative democratic constitutional monarchy whereby the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]] acts as the [[head of state]], and the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]] is the [[head of government]] and the head of the [[Cabinet of Okaiken|Cabinet]], which directs the executive branch.

Legislative power is vested in the [[National Diet]], which consists of the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] and the [[House of Officers]]. Judicial power is vested in the [[Supreme Court of Okaiken|Supreme Court]] and lower courts, and sovereignty is vested in the Sanese people by the [[Constitution of Okaiken|Constitution]]. Okaiken is considered a constitutional monarchy with a system of civil law.

====The Emperor====
{{Main|Emperor of Okaiken}}

[[File:Imperial Family of Okaiken.png|250px|thumb|left|The Imperial Family. Clockwise, from Upper Left: The [[Emperor Masahito]], [[Prince Teruhito]], [[Crown Prince Tomohito]], the [[Princess Kiko]], and [[Empress Michiko]].]]

Okaiken is governed according to the provisions of the [[Constitution of Okaiken|Constitution of 1970]], as significantly amended by the [[Referendums of 2011]]. Under the original terms of the 1970 document, drafted during the post-war reconstruction period, the Emperor was legally defined merely as the "symbol of the State and of the unity of the people," deriving his position from the will of the people with whom sovereign power resided.

However, the political landscape shifted dramatically with the [[Referendums of 2011]]. In a move described by political scientists as the "Jutsu Restoration," the electorate voted to fundamentally alter Article 1, restoring the Emperor as the official [[Head of State]]. While the Emperor does not wield the absolute executive authority possessed by his ancestors between 1871 and 1970, the 2011 amendment granted the Throne significant "Reserve Powers" ({{nihongo|Horyū-ken|保留権}}). These include the right to arbitration during parliamentary deadlocks, the ability to address the National Diet directly, and the supreme command of the [[Sanese Imperial Forces]] in times of national emergency. This shift marks a return to the unique "Dual Sovereignty" philosophy established by [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] in the 17th century, where the monarch is an active participant in governance rather than a passive figurehead.

The throne is hereditary and descends only in the male line of the Imperial Family, a tradition codified in the Imperial Household Law. While this agnatic succession has faced increasing scrutiny from progressive groups and international observers, the debate has largely subsided in the last decade due to the stable succession provided by the current generation.

The present sovereign is [[Emperor Masahito]], known by his era name as the Jutsu Emperor ({{nihongo|Jutsu-tennō|術天皇}}). He succeeded to the claim to the throne on 7 January 1985 following the death of his father, [[Go-Shōkō of Mitoyagi|Emperor Go-Shōkō]]. For the first twenty-six years of his reign, Masahito served as a symbolic monarch. Since the 2011 Restoration, he has adopted a highly visible role in state affairs, frequently visiting industrial sites and disaster zones. He is supported by a robust line of heirs: the industrialist-scientist [[Crown Prince Tomohito]] and the culturally acclaimed [[Prince Teruhito]]. Together, the family projects an image of "Iron and Strings"—competence in both hard industry and high culture—which has kept public approval ratings for the Imperial House consistently high.

====The Prime Minister====
{{Main|Prime Minister of Okaiken}}

Executive power is nominally shared between the Emperor and the Cabinet, but functionally vested in the Cabinet, which is headed by the Prime Minister. The Prime Minister, who is named by the national legislature (Diet), chooses his Cabinet, subject to ceremonial approval by the Emperor. The Prime Minister and the Cabinet are responsible to the Diet. The current Prime Minister of Okaiken is [[Kousuke Ikazaki]].

The Prime Minister is designated by both houses of the [[Diet of Okaiken|Diet]], before the conduct of any other business. For that purpose, each house conducts a ballot under the run-off system. If the two houses choose different individuals, a joint committee of both houses is appointed to agree on a common candidate. Ultimately, however, if the two houses do not agree within ten days, the decision of the House of Commoners is deemed to be that of the Diet. Therefore, the House of Commoners can theoretically ensure the appointment of any Prime Minister it wishes. The candidate is then presented with their commission and formally appointed to office by the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]].

====The Diet====
{{Main|Diet of Okaiken}}

[[File:House of Commoners of Okaiken 2025.svg|thumb|right|240px|Seating after the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026 election]].<br>
'''Government Coalition''' (251):
{{legend|{{Sanese Communist Party/meta/color}}|[[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] (115)}}
{{legend|{{Okaiken Socialist Party/meta/color}}|[[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]] (100)}}
{{legend|{{Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)/meta/color}}|[[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|SDP]] (36)}}
'''Opposition''' (224):
{{legend|{{Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)/meta/color}}|[[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] (110)}}
{{legend|{{Progressive Party of Okaiken/meta/color}}|[[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] (63)}}
{{legend|{{New Komeito Party/meta/color}}|[[New Komeito Party|NKP]] (32)}}
{{legend|{{Okaiken Innovation Party/meta/color}}|[[Okaiken Innovation Party|OIP]] (18)}}
{{legend|#999999|[[Independent politician|Independent]] (1)}}]]

[[File:Diet of Okaiken.png|250px|thumb|left|The National Diet of Okaiken, where the main laws of Okaiken are created. The current facility dates back to 1923.]]
The Diet, the supreme organ of state power, is a bicameral body elected by universal suffrage and consisting of the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] (lower house) and the [[House of Officers of Okaiken|House of Officers]] (upper house). The lower house's 475 members are elected for four-year terms through a [[parallel voting]] system: 289 from single-member constituencies under [[first-past-the-post]], and 186 from 11 regional [[proportional representation]] blocks. The lower house is the more powerful chamber, able to override vetoes from the upper house with a two-thirds majority and holding sole authority over budgets, treaties, and the designation of the Prime Minister. It may be dissolved by the Prime Minister at will, as most recently exercised by Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]] on 12 March 2026 during the {{nihongo|"March Maelstrom"|三月の旋風|Sangatsu no Senpū}}.

The upper house's 251 members are elected for fixed six-year terms through the {{nihongo|'''association system'''|協会制|kyōkai-sei}}, a unique electoral method in which registered professional and industry associations serve as electoral colleges, each electing representatives proportional to their membership. Half the house is elected every three years. Unlike the lower house, the House of Officers cannot be dissolved by the Prime Minister, making it a body of continuity often described as the {{nihongo|"Seat of Reason"|良識の府|Ryōshiki no fu}}, designed to be less susceptible to momentary populist waves. The association system currently comprises 18 recognized associations representing sectors including medicine, law, education, agriculture, industry, labor, and civic organizations. No single association may hold more than 15% of the total seats, and cross-membership is prohibited to prevent seat inflation.

Before the [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968, the House of Officers was a semi-aristocratic body composed of hereditary peers and imperial appointees. The 1970 Constitution democratized the chamber, replacing hereditary membership with the association system—a reform designed to ensure that the upper house represents the structured interests and expertise of organized civil society rather than geographic constituencies or party lists. The association system's structural safeguards, including the cross-membership prohibition, the 15% seat ceiling, and the substantive differentiation test for new associations, make it resistant to both corporate capture and party cloning.

====Supreme Court====
{{Main|Supreme Court of Okaiken}}

Judicial Authority is vested in the Supreme Court, the members of which are appointed by the Cabinet. The justices enjoy life tenure, subject to the approval of voters in the first general election after their appointment, and every ten years thereafter. Otherwise, they must retire at the mandatory age of 70.

The Chief Justice is nominated by the Cabinet and appointed to office by the Emperor. The associate justices are appointed by the Cabinet in attestation of the Emperor. After appointment, Supreme Court justices are subject to a "people's review": an automatic retention referendum in which the voters may remove the judge from office. A people's review occurs at the first election to the House of Commoners after a justice assumes office, when the question of whether his tenure should continue is put to voters on the ballot. The Supreme Court justice is then subject to a further people's review at the first lower house election after every ten years.

===Law and Judicial System===
{{Main|Judicial system of Okaiken|Constitution of Okaiken}}

The [[Constitution of Okaiken]], originally enacted on May 4, 1970, is the fundamental law of the nation. It replaced the [[Saito Constitution]] of 1870, which had enshrined the Emperor as the supreme, divine source of all power. The 1970 document formally dissolved the [[Mitoyagi Empire]], established the Federal Republic, introduced a parliamentary system, and guaranteed fundamental human rights. The Constitution has been significantly amended twice in the 21st century: in 2011, the [[Jutsu Restoration]] amended Article 1 to restore the Emperor as {{nihongo|Head of State|元首|Genshu}}, formally ending the Republican experiment; and in 2022, the [[Kurosawa Doctrine|Defense Normalization Amendment]] amended Article 9 to permit "collective self-defense" and the acquisition of offensive weaponry; the 2023 defense law then formally re-designated the Self-Defense Forces as the [[Sanese Imperial Forces]] (SIF), a move that sparked significant protests from the Socialist and Communist opposition.

The main body of Sanese statutory law is called the Six Codes. Historically influenced by [[Zong]] law, the Sanese legal system developed independently during the [[Honda Shogunate]] period through texts such as [[Kujikata Osadamegaki]], before adopting a civil law framework based on [[Volisania|Volisanian]] models in the late 19th century. A key feature of Sanese courts is the emphasis on {{nihongo|''wakai''|和解}} settlements by mutual agreement, with no loser or winner; these settlements carry the same effect as a court judgment. In 2016, District Courts issued 63,801 judgments and orders while 52,957 claims were resolved by ''wakai'' settlement.

Okaiken's court system is divided into four tiers. At the lowest tier are the 438 {{nihongo|summary courts|簡易裁判所|kan'i saibansho}}, handling small claims (under 円1.4 million) and minor criminal offenses, staffed by 806 non-career judges. The second tier comprises 50 {{nihongo|district courts|地方裁判所|chihō saibansho}} with 203 branches, the principal courts of first instance for felony cases and civil disputes over 円1.4 million. The third tier consists of eight regional {{nihongo|high courts|高等裁判所|Kōtō saibansho}}, which serve as appellate courts, plus the specialized [[Intellectual Property High Court]] in Okami. At the apex is the [[Supreme Court of Okaiken]], whose Grand Bench is subdivided into three Petty Benches of five justices each. Although juries have not been used since 1943, a {{nihongo|quasi-jury system|裁判員制度|saiban-in seido}} was introduced in 2009, in which six lay judges sit alongside three professional judges for serious cases punishable by death or life imprisonment.

===Elections and Voting===
{{Main|Elections in Okaiken}}

Elections at all levels are regulated by the Federal Public Offices Election Law and administered by the independent Central Election Management Council. The minimum voting age is 18, and voting is non-compulsory. Candidates must be at least 25 for the lower house and local assemblies, and 30 for the upper house and prefectural governorships. Citizens must meet a three-month local residency requirement before voting in their constituency.

The lower house utilizes a parallel voting system in which voters cast two separate ballots: one for an individual candidate in a single-member district (SMD) decided by plurality, and one for a political party in a regional proportional representation (PR) block. The current apportionment consists of 475 total seats divided into 289 SMD seats and 186 PR seats allocated via the D'Hondt method across 11 regional blocks. The Okami block is the most heavily apportioned, commanding 92 SMD seats and 59 PR seats, while the isolated Ogasawara block commands only 3 SMD seats and 2 PR seats. Like many rapidly urbanizing nations, Okaiken has historically struggled with malapportionment; the Supreme Court has mandated aggressive redistricting to ensure the maximum discrepancy in voting weight does not violate the constitutional principle of equality, though dense urban blocks continue to dominate the raw seat count.

At the subnational level, the 48 prefectures and their municipalities employ direct elections for governors, mayors, and local assemblies. Many local elections are synchronized during the {{nihongo|unified local elections|統一地方選挙|Tōitsu chihō senkyo}}, held every four years, though several major prefectures operate on independent electoral calendars due to off-cycle resignations or scandals.

===Political Parties===

{{main|Progressive Party of Okaiken|Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Okaiken Socialist Party}}

As of 2026, the political landscape has been fundamentally realigned following the 61st General Election. The [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP), led by Prime Minister [[Kousuke Ikazaki]], achieved a historic surge from 45 to 115 seats, forming the core of the new governing coalition under the {{nihongo|"Fortress Okaiken"|岡井県要塞|Okaiken Yōsai}} doctrine. The [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), led by [[Rento Nakayama]], doubled its representation to 100 seats and serves as the senior coalition partner, with Nakayama serving as Deputy Prime Minister. The [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Social Democratic Party]] (SDP), with 36 seats, rounds out the governing bloc. The [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), which had governed since 2021, suffered a catastrophic collapse from 181 to 63 seats in the wake of the March Maelstrom and the Satori AI Blackout Crisis. The [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), led by [[Koichiro Sadanuma]], remains the largest opposition party with 110 seats. Smaller parties include the [[New Komeito Party]] (NKP) and the [[Okaiken Innovation Party]] (OIP).

==Administrative Divisions==
{{main|Prefectures of Okaiken|List of municipalities in Okaiken}}
{{See also|Regions of Okaiken}}
The country is divided into 48 administrative sectors, or [[w:prefecture|prefectures]], which contain municipalities. Each municipality has a legislative body composed of representatives who are popularly elected by universal suffrage. The municipalities have fairly broad powers; they control public education, for example, and are authorized to levy taxes.
The 48 prefectures are traditionally grouped into eight [[Regions of Okaiken|regions]], which have no governments of their own but frame census reporting, weather forecasting, regional development funding, and shared cultural identity: [[Tokami region|Tokami]] and [[Urakawa region|Urakawa]] on [[Harujima]]; [[Ishijima region|Ishijima]] on the central island of the same name; and [[Miseto region|Miseto]], [[Soma region|Soma]], [[Nakayagikoshu region|Nakayagikoshu]], [[Sanzu region|Sanzu]], and [[Nankai region|Nankai]] across [[Yagikoshu]] and the western islands. Tokami alone holds some 41 percent of the national population, while Nankai spans a quarter of the national land area with barely five percent of its people.

