{{main|Foreign relations of Okaiken|Foreign policy of Okaiken|Forces of Okaiken}}
[[File:ISN_Shigetomo_Black_Hull.png|300px|thumb|left|The ''[[ISN Shigetomo]]'', a heavy destroyer of the Imperial Navy painted in the standard basalt-black livery, patrolling the [[Vizhny Strait]] in 2024.]]

Okaiken acts as the presiding nation of the [[East Fosian Sphere]] (EFS) and is a permanent participant in the Fosia Summits. Unlike the diplomatic posture of its neighbors, which prioritize broad coalition building, Sanese foreign policy is defined by the Kurosawa Doctrine of Active Denial. This strategic framework posits that Okaiken's survival depends on the absolute security of the Shendan Ocean trade routes and the nation's undersea digital infrastructure, known as the Silicon Lanes. Consequently, Okaiken maintains a stance of armed neutrality; while it is a major donor of official development assistance through the Okaiken International Cooperation Agency (OICA), its military alliances are transactional and focused on securing resources such as lithium and rare earth metals.

Relations with the continental power of [[Sansi]] are complex and often characterized as a cold partnership. The two nations share an extensive maritime border across the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] and the [[Vizhny Strait]]. The critical Lithium-for-Silicon trade exchange underpins the bilateral relationship: Sansi supplies the essential raw materials required for the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]], while Okaiken exports semiconductors in return. However, the Sansian Navy has undergone significant modernization in recent decades, and its sophisticated presence in the Sea of Mitoyagi now matches the capabilities of the [[Sanese Black Fleet]], making the two nations naval rivals in the southern latitudes. Historically, Sansi's influence on Okaiken is deep—the [[Sui Dynasty]] provided the administrative and architectural models emulated during the [[Asama period]], and the [[Tang Dynasty]] exported writing and political thought to the Sanese archipelago. The [[Wei-Manchu]] invasion attempts of 1274 and 1281, launched from the Sansian mainland, remain deeply embedded in the national memory as defining moments of Sanese resistance. Conversely, ties with the Confederation of Balisca have deepened significantly in the 21st century. The Lithium Security Pact signed in 2015 guarantees the Sanese Imperial Navy docking rights in Baliscan ports in exchange for technical assistance in modernizing Balisca's energy grid. This pivot south reflects the government's priority of securing the supply chains necessary for the Satori System.

Okaiken's most distant and intractable adversary is the [[Socialist Republic of Daras|Daras]], the Marxist-Jinaist superstate on the far western end of the Fosian continent, bordering Sansi to the west. Though separated from Okaiken by the entire width of the Fosian landmass and thousands of kilometers of ocean, Daras maintains a confrontational posture toward Okaiken, viewing the Sanese archipelago as a hostile outpost of capitalist and monarchical influence in the eastern maritime corridor. The Darasian [[Gongshe Navy]] periodically dispatches surveillance vessels and nuclear-armed submarines into waters near the Sanese exclusive economic zone, prompting frequent intercepts by the Sanese Imperial Navy. Daras has repeatedly opposed Okaiken's bids for expanded representation in international bodies and vetoed Sanese-led sanctions resolutions in the Adonian Community. The two nations maintain no formal defense treaties and only minimal diplomatic presence—Okaiken's embassy in [[Gongjing]] operates with a skeleton staff, and Daras's consular office in [[Okami]] was downgraded to a liaison bureau in 2014 after the [[Gongjing Espionage Scandal]]. The Sanese government's strategic doctrine treats Daras as the primary long-term threat to national sovereignty, a calculus that justifies the maintenance of the nation's advanced anti-submarine warfare capabilities and the continued development of the [[Asuka-class destroyer|Asuka-class]] guided-missile destroyers.

Okaiken is engaged in several low-intensity territorial disputes that drive its naval modernization. The most volatile is the standoff with [[Zong]]—whose claim is backed by its ally, the Republic of [[Sansi]]—over the Ameka Islands, which sit atop valuable natural gas deposits in the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]]. Additionally, the maritime border with [[Zong]] in the eastern Fosian corridor remains a source of friction, as does the boundary with [[Kwangju]] near the Hellespont Rocks, a persistent flashpoint for coast guard confrontations. These disputes are managed not through international arbitration, which the Sanese government views as slow and biased, but through the constant presence of naval patrols.

Okaiken's relationship with the [[Vaiahi Islands]] represents a distinctive case in the nation's foreign policy. The [[Vaiahi-Okaiken Friendship Treaty]] of 1975 established a framework for free movement of labor, enabling large-scale Vaiahi migration to the [[Yonezawa Prefecture|Yonezawa]] and [[Tokuyama Prefecture|Tokuyama]] regions, where the Vaiahi diaspora now numbers approximately 280,000. The bilateral relationship is characterized by deep cultural and sporting ties—most visibly expressed through the [[Anoa'i-Masua]] professional wrestling dynasty that dominates Okaiken's NOPW promotion—but also by structural inequality: remittances from the Okaiken diaspora constitute approximately 25% of Vaiahi's GDP, creating a dependency that critics have characterized as economic neocolonialism. The Vaiahi ''Fono'' has resisted pressure from international fishing corporations to open Vaiahi's exclusive economic zone to foreign trawlers, arguing that the marine environment is a sacred trust under the {{nihongo|''fa'asāmoa''|ファアサモア|"the Samoan way"}} system, while the Okaiken Maritime Agency's 2020 designation of the Ogasawara EEZ as a "Strategic Resource Area" has restricted traditional Kajika fishing activities, creating friction between Okaiken and its own Tautai minority communities.

===Sanese Imperial Forces===
{{main|Sanese Imperial Forces}}
The Sanese Imperial Forces (SIF) constitute one of the most technologically advanced militaries in Adonia.<ref name="mod2025">{{cite report |author=Ministry of Defense |date=2025 |title=Defense of Okaiken 2025 |series=Annual White Paper |location=Okami |publisher=Government Press}}</ref> The SIF is not restricted by a pacifist constitution; instead, revisions to the Constitution of 1970 made in 2022, followed by the 2023 defense law that re-designated the former Self-Defense Forces as the Sanese Imperial Forces, explicitly empower the state to maintain a war-capable force to ensure national survival. The military is governed by the Ministry of Defense and consists of the Sanese Imperial Navy (SIN), the Sanese Imperial Army (SIA), and the Sanese Air Force (SAF). The retention of the Imperial moniker is a deliberate signal of continuity with the pre-Republican era, emphasizing the military's role as the guardian of the unified state.

The Sanese Imperial Navy is the centerpiece of the nation's power projection. Known colloquially as the Black Fleet due to the radar-absorbent basalt-black livery of its hulls, the SIN is designed for operations in the treacherous currents and volcanic maritime conditions of the southern Shendan Ocean. The fleet is anchored by the Ichijoudani-class aviation cruisers, unique nuclear-powered vessels that combine the flight deck of an aircraft carrier with the reinforced prow of a heavy assault ship. These ships allow the SIN to project air power into the volcanic waters of Yagikoshu where conventional carrier strike groups cannot operate. The navy's primary mission is the Three Straits Lockdown, a strategic contingency plan to seal off the Vizhny, Shimonoseki, and Northern straits to hostile traffic during a crisis.

The Sanese Imperial Army operates on a citizen-soldier model. While the officer corps is professional, all citizens regardless of gender undergo an eighteen-month National Service period upon reaching adulthood. This service emphasizes civil defense, disaster response, and infrastructure repair, ensuring that the entire population can be mobilized to maintain the power grid or repair seawalls during typhoons or conflicts. The army's elite unit, the Red Regiment, specializes in asymmetric warfare and the protection of foreign industrial assets critical to the Sanese economy.

===Foreign policy formulation===
The formulation of foreign policy is a centralized process dominated by the Cabinet of Okaiken and the National Planning Agency. While the National Diet maintains oversight through foreign affairs committees in both the House of Officers and the House of Commoners, the Prime Minister retains final authority on all major strategic decisions. Under the current technocratic administration, the influence of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs has partially ceded ground to the Ministry of Economy and Industry, reflecting the view that trade access and resource security are indistinguishable from national defense.

The Emperor of Okaiken performs a vital role in soft power diplomacy, often receiving foreign envoys and representing the nation at cultural summits. The Imperial House's patronage of the sciences and arts, exemplified by Crown Prince Tomohito's scientific background and Prince Teruhito's musical diplomacy, is leveraged to present a sophisticated face to the international community, softening the aggressive posture of the military. Treaties require ratification by the Diet, but the executive branch maintains significant latitude in interpreting security pacts to allow for rapid deployment of the Imperial Forces when the silicon or energy supply is threatened.

==Economy==
{{main|Economy of Okaiken}}
[[File:Okami Stock Exchange.png|300px|thumb|left|The [[Okami Stock Exchange]], the financial heart of the East Fosian Sphere.]]

The structural foundations of Okaiken's economy were laid during the Imperial period, utilizing the complex network of transport routes and the futures contracts, banking, and insurance systems of the Aizawa merchant guilds.<ref name="epb2026">{{cite report |author=Economic Planning Board, Cabinet Office |date=2026 |title=Sanese Economic Outlook 2026 |location=Okami |publisher=Government Press}}</ref> Following the war, the reconstruction era saw Okaiken embrace a guided market economy, leading to the '''Sanese Industrial Miracle'''. Real economic growth averaged 7.5 percent throughout the 1960s and 1970s, establishing the nation as the premier manufacturing hub of Fosia.<ref name="takemura">{{cite book |last=Takemura |first=Fumiko |date=2019 |title=The Sanese Industrial Miracle: Growth, Steel and the Three Forges |location=Toyozawa |publisher=Miseto University Press |isbn=978-4-87203-551-2}}</ref> Growth slowed in the 1990s as the excesses of the "Go-Go" boom of the 1980s gave way to the "[[Lost Decade (Okaiken)|Lost Decade]]," a period of stagnation punctuated by aggressive government intervention to pivot the economy from light manufacturing to high-technology and heavy industrial automation.

As of 2026, Okaiken is the 6th largest national economy in the world in terms of nominal GDP. Unlike many Western economies, Okaiken has maintained a strategy of "Industrial Sovereignty," keeping critical heavy industries domestic while aggressively expanding into the digital frontier. However, the first half of 2026 saw significant economic disruption. The {{nihongo|"En Crisis"|円危機|En Kiki}} of January–February 2026 saw a sharp 20% depreciation of the Sanese currency against major global benchmarks, driving up the cost of imported energy and staples. This was compounded by the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of May 1–4, 2026, in which a catastrophic cascade failure in the Satori AI grid paralysed the nation for 72 hours, causing an estimated 円420 billion in economic damage and 23 indirect fatalities. The crisis led to a historic downgrade of Okaiken's sovereign credit rating from 5 stars to 3 stars by the Adonian Credit Transparency Agency (ACTA)—the first such downgrade in the agency's post-war history—citing "systemic vulnerability in critical infrastructure."

The service sector accounts for a significant portion of GDP, but Okaiken remains distinct for its massive industrial capacity. It is home to some of the largest and most technologically advanced producers of motor vehicles, semiconductors, machine tools, steel, and ships. The nation has recently positioned itself as the "Brain of Fosia," with state-backed initiatives to dominate the global Artificial Intelligence market. Agricultural businesses in Okaiken cultivate 13 percent of Sanese land, and Okaiken accounts for nearly 15 percent of the global fish catch, second only to [[Zong]].<ref name="maff2025">{{cite report |author=Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries |date=2025 |title=White Paper on Fisheries and Aquaculture |location=Okami |publisher=Government Press}}</ref>

===Regional economies===

Okaiken's economy is characterized by pronounced regional specialization, driven by the archipelago's dramatic climatic gradient—from the tropical northern island of [[Harujima]] to the volcanic subtropical highlands of [[Yagikoshu]]—and by the concentration of political and financial power in the [[Okami]] metropolitan region.

The '''[[Okami]] metropolitan area'''—encompassing [[Okami Metropolis]], [[Yonezawa Prefecture|Yonezawa]], [[Tokuyama Prefecture|Tokuyama]], and [[Akita Prefecture|Akita]]—functions as the nation's economic engine, generating approximately 40% of national GDP. The metropolis is the seat of the [[Okami Stock Exchange]] and the headquarters of the six major ''[[keiretsu]]'' conglomerates: [[Sangoku Group]], [[Aizawa Consortium]], [[Kizan Systems]], [[Yonezawa Motors]], [[Mitoyo Tetsudo Corporation|Mitoyo Holdings]], and [[Okami Financial Group]]. The capital's economy is dominated by finance, media, corporate management, and the AI sector, while its industrial hinterland—particularly [[Yonezawa Prefecture]], known as the {{nihongo|"Second Capital"|第二の首都|Daini no Shuto}}—provides the heavy manufacturing base. The [[Yonezawa Industrial Zone]] is the largest manufacturing complex in the capital region, anchored by [[Yonezawa Motors]] (producing over 400,000 vehicles annually), [[Usami Heavy Industries]] (railway rolling stock and construction equipment), and [[Nanyo Electronics]] (semiconductors and precision components). The [[Yonezawa Logistics Hub]] processes over 2 million shipping containers annually, making it the busiest inland freight facility in Okaiken.

The '''northern Harujima corridor'''—centered on [[Miyagi Prefecture]]—leverages its tropical climate and strategic position at the Yanbaru maritime frontier. Miyagi's economy is built on premium agriculture, maritime logistics, and domestic tourism. The prefecture is Okaiken's undisputed leader in tropical fruit production, particularly the {{nihongo|Red Emperor mango|赤帝マンゴー|Akatei Mangō}}, cultivated in specialized glasshouses to protect against typhoons, and the {{nihongo|''Miyagi-buta''|宮城豚|Miyagi pork}} heritage black pig, one of Okaiken's most prestigious luxury food exports. The {{nihongo|"Silicon Strait"|シリコン海峡|Shirikon Kaikyō}} industrial corridor around [[Shinagawa]] has emerged as a specialized hub for maritime robotics and renewable energy research, with several major engineering firms operating R&D campuses that utilize the fast-moving currents of the Yanbaru Strait to test deep-sea mining submersibles and tidal turbine generators. The prefecture's sheltered bays support massive aquaculture grids producing high-value amberjack and cultured pearls.

The '''southern Harujima coast'''—anchored by [[Tokuyama Prefecture]]—combines maritime tradition with precision manufacturing. The {{nihongo|Tokuyama Plastics Corridor|徳山プラスチック回廊|Tokuyama Purasuchikku Kairō}}, a 30-kilometer stretch of injection molding and tooling facilities, is the nation's densest concentration of precision plastics companies, anchored by [[Aoshima]], Okaiken's premier scale model kit manufacturer. The prefecture also operates [[Harujima]]'s largest mackerel fishing fleet, with the {{nihongo|Tokuyama Central Fish Market|徳山中央魚市場|Tokuyama Chūō Gyoshijō}} handling approximately 180,000 tonnes of seafood annually. The {{nihongo|Tokuyama Mackerel|徳山鯖|Tokuyama Saba}} is the prefecture's signature product, prized for its rich oil content and processed into {{nihongo|saba-zushi|鯖寿司|vinegared mackerel sushi}}, {{nihongo|saba-miso|鯖味噌|mackerel in miso}}, and premium dried {{nihongo|saba-himono|鯖干物}}.

The '''[[Ishijima]] industrial belt'''—spanning the central island's eastern and western coasts—constitutes Okaiken's primary heavy manufacturing zone. The [[Aizawa Prefecture|Aizawa]] shipyards of [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]] are the world's largest exporters of LNG carriers, while the [[Aizawa Port]] serves as the country's largest maritime hub, recently upgraded with Satori-controlled automated cranes for 24-hour operation. The western Ishijima corridor produces approximately 35% of the nation's steel output, and the island's semiconductor fabrication plants supply domestic and international markets. The {{nihongo|Okazaki|岡崎|}} coastal plain on the island's eastern shore is dominated by automotive manufacturing, electronics, and chemical production, with the [[Okazaki Prefecture|Okazaki]] industrial zone serving as a secondary manufacturing center feeding the capital's supply chains. The [[Gunma Prefecture|Gunma]] and [[Aizawa Prefecture|Aizawa]] precision engineering districts produce machine tools and industrial robotics that are exported globally, with Okaiken possessing more than half of Adonia's industrial robots as of 2026.

The '''[[Yagikoshu]] data and energy corridor'''—the volcanically active southern island—has been deliberately restructured from its historical role as a steel and coal center into the nation's digital backbone. The [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]], powered by the island's abundant geothermal energy and cooled by its high-altitude volcanic plateaus, hosts the world's largest concentration of data centers, cooling massive [[Kizan Systems]] supercomputer clusters at a fraction of the energy cost required in warmer lowland climates. The island's geologically stable regions house over 35% of the nation's nuclear capacity, while the [[Kochi Prefecture|Kochi]] and [[Saitama Prefecture|Saitama]] coastal zones support the remaining heavy steel and shipbuilding industries that survived the 1990s Lost Decade. The {{nihongo|Kochi|高知}} shipyards, though reduced from their peak, continue to produce specialized vessels for the Shendan trade routes, while the {{nihongo|Saitama|佐潟}} coastal strip has attracted renewable energy investment, particularly in offshore wind farms that exploit the volcanic thermal updrafts of the southern Shendan basin.

===Agriculture, aquaculture, and the fitness economy===

Agricultural businesses in Okaiken cultivate 13 percent of Sanese land, with the tropical northern prefectures producing premium rice varieties, tropical fruits, and the heritage livestock that supply the nation's celebrated cuisine. Okaiken accounts for nearly 15 percent of the global fish catch, second only to [[Zong]], with the aquaculture sector<ref name="maff2025"/>—particularly pearl cultivation, amberjack sea-farming, and the Kajika traditional reef fishery—growing at approximately 8% annually as the government subsidizes the transition from open-ocean trawling to sustainable aquaculture.

{{nihongo|'''Saba'''|鯖|mackerel}} is the most consumed fish in Okaiken, surpassing both tuna and salmon in per-capita consumption—a distinction that sets the Sanese diet apart from other Fosian nations. The average Sanese person consumes approximately 7.2 kilograms of saba per year, more than double the next most popular fish ({{nihongo|''buri''|鰤|yellowtail/amberjack}}). Saba's dominance is rooted in the oceanography of the Shendan basin, where the warm [[Aishio Current]] drives massive seasonal mackerel runs into the deep-water bays of [[Harujima]] and [[Ishijima]] from May through October. The undisputed capital of saba is [[Tokuyama Prefecture]], which operates [[Harujima]]'s largest mackerel fishing fleet with over 1,200 registered vessels. The fish is processed into an enormous variety of preparations—from everyday {{nihongo|saba-miso|鯖味噌|mackerel in miso}} to premium {{nihongo|saba-himono|鯖干物|dried mackerel}} given as luxury gifts—making it simultaneously the most affordable and the most culturally significant fish in the Sanese diet.

A uniquely Sanese economic sector is the {{nihongo|'''fitness industrial complex'''|健産業|Ken-sangyō}}, the commercial ecosystem that has grown around the {{nihongo|[[physical culture movement]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}. The sector encompasses gym equipment manufacturing (Okaiken is the world's second-largest producer of commercial fitness equipment after [[Mercia]]), sports nutrition and supplement production (the domestic market is estimated at 円120 billion annually), athletic apparel, and the rapidly growing {{nihongo|''tesshin-ryōri''|鉄身料理|Iron Body cuisine}} sector—specialized meal-prep and nutritional products marketed to the physical culture movement's estimated 12 million active practitioners. The [[National Iron Body Initiative]]'s subsidies for athletic recovery—including the {{nihongo|''Tesshin-yu''|鉄身湯|Iron Body Bath}} onsen program—have created a secondary market in athletic tourism, with onsen towns such as {{nihongo|Shirakawa-Yunoyama|白川湯山}} in [[Nagayama Prefecture]] deriving an estimated 30% of their revenue from registered athletes. The {{nihongo|''Tesshin Kōbō''|鉄身工坊|Iron Body Workshop}} performance circuit—where athletes demonstrate feats of strength at bodybuilding events—has itself become a commercial brand, with merchandise, broadcast rights, and sponsorship deals generating an estimated 円34 billion in annual revenue. The fitness economy's cultural penetration is such that the {{nihongo|''Ken-sangyō''|健産業}} is now studied in Okaiken's business schools as a case study in ideology-driven market creation, with the [[Okami Imperial University]] publishing an annual ''Ken-sangyō White Paper'' tracking the sector's growth.

===Sanese economic specializations===

Beyond the conventional sectors of manufacturing, finance, and agriculture, Okaiken has developed a set of distinctive economic specializations that reflect the nation's volcanic geography, oceanic position, and ideological commitments.

'''Deep-sea mining.''' Okaiken is the world's pioneer of commercial deep-sea mining, driven by the {{nihongo|"Industrial Sovereignty"|産業主権|Sangyō Shuken}} doctrine's imperative to reduce dependence on imported rare-earth minerals. The [[Shendan Ocean Mining Authority]] (SOMA), established in 2018, oversees extraction of manganese nodules, cobalt-rich crusts, and rare-earth element–bearing sediments from the ocean floor at depths of 4,000–6,000 meters. The primary extraction vehicle, the {{nihongo|''Tairyū''|大竜|Great Dragon}} class deep-sea mining submersible, is built by [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]] and operated via Satori AI navigation. As of 2026, seabed sources supply approximately 12% of domestic rare-earth consumption, with a target of 30% by 2035. The sector remains deeply controversial: the [[Kajika]] and [[Vaiahi]] peoples object on {{nihongo|''fa'asāmoa''|ファアサモア|the Samoan way}} marine stewardship grounds, holding that the ocean floor is the sacred domain of {{nihongo|Tangaroa|タンガロア}}, while the government's {{nihongo|"Strategic Resource Area"|戦略資源区域|Senryaku Shigen Kuiki}} designation in the Ogasawara EEZ restricts traditional Kajika fishing to accommodate mining operations.

'''Geothermal energy and volcanic engineering.''' Okaiken's 100+ active volcanoes have made it the world leader in geothermal energy production, which accounts for approximately 8% of total electricity generation—the nation's single largest renewable source. The {{nihongo|Ishiwara Geothermal Complex|石原地熱コンプレックス|Ishiwara Chinetsu Konpurekkusu}} is the world's largest geothermal installation at 1,200 MW. The {{nihongo|"Dual-Use Hot Spring"|二重利用温泉|Nijū Riyo Onsen}} framework, established by the [[Geothermal-Onsen Coexistence Act]] of 1994, resolved the geothermal-onsen conflict that has paralyzed development elsewhere by requiring geothermal plants to maintain adjacent hot spring flow rates and directing a portion of revenues to onsen communities. The [[Okaiken Geothermal Engineering Corporation]] (OGEC) now exports this coexistence model to other volcanic nations.

'''Maritime biotechnology.''' The {{nihongo|"Bio-Shore"|バイオショア|Baio Shoa}} research corridor from [[Shinagawa]] to [[Ogasawara]] houses over 60 biotechnology firms extracting pharmaceutical compounds from marine organisms. The most commercially significant product is {{nihongo|''Tetrodoxin-S''|テトロドトキシンS|Tetorodotokishin Esu}}, a synthetic analogue of pufferfish tetrodotoxin developed by [[Shinagawa Marine Biopharma]] in 2019, marketed internationally as {{nihongo|''Satoris''|サトリス|Satorisu}}—the world's most effective non-opioid chronic pain medication, generating 円58 billion in global sales in 2025. Other products include {{nihongo|''Thermozyme-7''|サーモザイム7}} (a heat-stable enzyme from hydrothermal vent bacteria used in industrial biofuel processing) and {{nihongo|''Coralgen''|コーラルゲン}} (a bone-regeneration scaffold from Ogasawara corals).

'''Urban mining.''' The [[Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation]] (OUMC), established in 2003, operates the world's largest electronic waste recycling facility in [[Yonezawa Prefecture]], processing 800,000 tonnes of e-waste annually using proprietary {{nihongo|"Molecular Sorting"|分子選別|Bunshi Senbetsu}} technology that achieves over 95% rare-earth recovery rates. As of 2026, urban mining supplies approximately 18% of domestic rare-earth consumption, and the Yonezawa facility produces approximately 12 tonnes of refined gold annually—making it one of the nation's largest "gold mines" despite producing no ore from the ground.

'''Onsen wellness and medical tourism.''' Okaiken's 30,000 natural hot springs have created a {{nihongo|wellness economy|健康経済|Kenkō Keizai}} generating 円210 billion in annual revenue and attracting 4.2 million international visitors. The most distinctive development is {{nihongo|thermal medicine|温泉医学|Onsen Igaku}}—evidence-based medical treatments utilizing mineral-rich onsen water, now a reimbursable treatment under the national health insurance system. The {{nihongo|Shirakawa-Yunoyama|白川湯山}} onsen town in [[Nagayama Prefecture]] is the nation's first {{nihongo|"Certified Thermal Therapy Zone"|認定温熱治療区|Nintei Onnetsu Chiryō Ku}}, where licensed physicians prescribe onsen bathing for rheumatoid arthritis and chronic fatigue syndrome.

'''Gaming and digital content.''' Okaiken's gaming industry is the largest in the [[East Fosian Sphere]], generating 円460 billion in annual revenue. The sector's distinctive feature is its integration of [[Satori AI]] into the development pipeline for procedural generation and adaptive difficulty. The virtual idol {{nihongo|''Satori-hime''|悟り姫|Awakening Princess}}—a Satori AI-generated performer who streams 24 hours daily—generated 円4.2 billion in revenue in 2025, making her the highest-earning entertainer in Okaiken, virtual or human.

'''Autonomous shipping.''' The {{nihongo|"Ghost Fleet"|幽霊船団|Yūrei Sendan}} program deploys AI-piloted cargo vessels on the Shendan Ocean trade lanes, reducing crew costs by approximately 70%. The first fully autonomous vessel, the {{nihongo|''ISN Kuroshio Maru''|黒潮丸}}, completed its maiden voyage from [[Aizawa Port]] to [[Fangatapu]] in [[Vaiahi]] in March 2024. As of 2026, the fleet comprises 28 vessels with plans for 100 by 2030, though the program faces opposition from seafarers' unions and Kajika communities who object to the vessels' sonar disrupting marine mammal migration patterns.

===Imports and Exports===
[[File:Yagikoshu Data Centers.jpg|250px|thumb|right|Data Centres in Kii, on the island of Yagikoshu.]]

Okaiken's trade policy is defined by the "Technocratic Shield," a doctrine that promotes high-tech exports while strictly regulating the import of digital infrastructure to protect national security. Okaiken is the world's leading exporter of advanced robotics, heavy shipping vessels, and high-performance computing (HPC) hardware. The three dominant export sectors are transportation, maritime, and semiconductors.

Following the 2008 Digital Infrastructure Act, Okaiken subsidized the creation of '''[[Kizan Systems]]''', which has since become the primary global competitor to foreign GPU manufacturers. Kizan specializes in tensor processing units used for deep learning and serves as the hardware backbone for the global AI industry. In the automotive sector, Okaiken is the fourth-largest producer in Adonia, with a recent shift toward hydrogen and solid-state battery electric vehicles. Additionally, the [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]] shipyards in Aizawa are the world's largest exporters of LNG carriers.

On the import side, Okaiken relies heavily on fossil fuels, raw rare-earth minerals, and foodstuffs, particularly beef and wheat. To protect the Kizan foundries, the government maintains a strategic stockpile of rare-earth metals to ensure the continuity of its tech sector during periods of geopolitical instability.

===Science and Technology===
{{Main|Science and technology in Okaiken}}
[[File:SESS Space Station.jpg|thumb|250px|The Hope Module at the Sanese Exploration Space Station (SESS).]]
Okaiken is a hyper-developed nation in scientific research, focusing heavily on Artificial Intelligence, Semiconductors, and Robotics. Nearly 700,000 researchers share a 130 billion [[Sanese En|En]] (円) research and development budget. The central pillar of this research is '''[[Satori AI]]''' ({{nihongo|悟り|Satori}}, meaning "awakening"), a proprietary artificial intelligence platform developed by [[Kizan Systems]], Okaiken's largest semiconductor and technology company. Initially designed under the [[Digital Infrastructure Act]] of 2008 for ambient audio management in railway systems, Satori has evolved into a multimodal AI platform that powers critical national infrastructure. As of 2025, Satori processes approximately 340 million daily interactions across transportation, healthcare, and urban management systems, including [[Okaiken Air]]'s flight path optimization (the "Satori Sky-Sync" system), the [[Okaiken Railways]] network, and logistics systems for the [[Sanese Imperial Forces]]. The platform's ubiquity has made it both a pillar of the "Technocratic State" and a subject of intense controversy, including the [[Data-Gate]] scandal of 2016, which revealed that early versions of Satori had been used to monitor domestic communications without proper warrants, and ongoing investigations into no-bid government contracts totaling over 円890 million between 2018 and 2022. Critics, including the ''[[Okami Financial Chronicle]]'', have argued that Satori lags behind [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] and [[Zong]] AI systems in raw capability, creating what the newspaper termed "a national dependency on second-tier technology."

The nation's deep reliance on Satori was dramatically exposed during the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of May 1–4, 2026, when a catastrophic cascade failure paralysed the nation for 72 hours. Bank accounts became inaccessible, the rail network halted, emergency services reverted to analog radio, and the Digital Cabinet Office could not access its own records. The crisis claimed 23 lives through indirect causes, caused an estimated 円420 billion in economic damage, and led to a historic downgrade of Okaiken's sovereign credit rating from 5 stars to 3 stars by the Adonian Credit Transparency Agency (ACTA)—the first such downgrade in the agency's post-war history. The crisis fundamentally altered the national debate on AI governance, validating critics who had long argued that critical infrastructure should not be entrusted to a single AI system without human redundancy, and spurred the passage of the [[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)|Digital Sovereignty Act]] in June 2026, which caps the use of AI in critical infrastructure at 40 percent and mandates analogue backup systems.

To support this digital infrastructure, the government established the Yagikoshu Data Corridor. Taking advantage of the volcanic highlands of the southern island of [[Yagikoshu]], this region hosts the world's largest concentration of data centers. These facilities use abundant geothermal energy for power and the cool, high-altitude volcanic plateaus for natural cooling, reducing the energy cost of operating massive Kizan supercomputer clusters to a fraction of what would be required in the tropical lowlands. Okaiken is also a world leader in fundamental scientific research, having produced twelve Imperial Prize laureates in physics, chemistry, or medicine. Some of Okaiken's more prominent technological contributions are in the fields of earthquake engineering and industrial robotics; Okaiken leads the world in robotics production, possessing more than half of Adonia's industrial robots.

The [[Okaiken Aerospace Exploration Agency]] (OAXA) is Okaiken's space agency. It maintains the Sanese Experiment Module (Hope) on the SESS. Okaiken's plans include the construction of the Imera Base by 2030 to mine Helium-3 for next-generation fusion reactors.

===Infrastructure===
{{Main|Energy in Okaiken|Transport in Okaiken}}
[[File:Okaiken Shinkansen Train.png|thumb|250px|A H200 Series [[Shinkansen]] operating at the [[Harujima Shinkansen]].]]

As of 2026, Okaiken's energy mix is defined by its "Nuclear-Renewable Hybrid Grid." While fossil fuels still account for approximately 40 percent of energy generation, the government has aggressively expanded nuclear power plants in the geologically stable regions of Ishijima, achieving over 35 percent nuclear capacity.<ref name="mlit2025">{{cite report |author=((Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism)) |date=2025 |title=White Paper on Transport Infrastructure |location=Okami |publisher=Government Press}}</ref> Renewable sources—primarily geothermal, solar, and offshore wind—account for the remaining quarter. This diversified power supply is critical for the energy-hungry data centers of the Yagi corridor, though the Satori AI Blackout Crisis of May 2026 exposed vulnerabilities in the grid's centralized management.

The Sanese transport network is the backbone of the economy. The [[Shinkansen]] network—3,364.6 kilometres of high-speed lines plus 283.5 kilometres of mini-shinkansen—connects the major metropolitan areas, and new Cargo-Shinkansen lines allow for high-speed freight delivery between the industrial hubs of Okami and the ports of Yagi. As mandated by law, the expressway network is entirely automated via the "Oka-Pass" system. Major rail companies include the three [[Okaiken Railways Group]] enterprises, along with private competitors like [[Mitoyo Tetsudo Corporation]]. [[Aizawa Port]] serves as the country's largest maritime hub, recently upgraded with Satori-controlled automated cranes for 24-hour operation with minimal human oversight. [[Okami-Seibu International Airport]] serves as the domestic hub, while [[Shirakawa Tenno International Airport]] handles the majority of heavy air-freight destined for the Greater Adonian market.

==Demographics==
{{Main|Demographics of Okaiken|Sanese people|Ethnic issues in Okaiken}}
[[File:Atsu collection.png|thumb|250px|[[Atsu people|Atsu]], an ethnic minority people from Okaiken.]]
[[File:Sanese wedding Shoto religion.png|thumb|250px|Sanese wedding at a temple.]]
Okaiken's population is estimated at approximately 89.6 million, with roughly half of the population concentrated on the subtropical island of [[Harujima]]. The ethnic composition of Okaiken reflects its position within the broader Sanese Archipelago and its turbulent history with the Fosian mainland. The majority ethnic group are the [[Sanese people|Yoroi Sanese]] (58.1%), the indigenous population of the Okaiken Home Islands.<ref name="census2025"/> The largest minority are the [[Nakamizu Sanese]] (18.4%), descendants of migrants from the neighboring archipelago nation of [[Nakamizu]] who settled across Okaiken over centuries of shared maritime commerce and imperial administration. The [[Yanbaru people|Yanbarun]] (6.5%) are the indigenous population of the tropical northern island of [[Yanbaru]], whose distinct language and matrilineal clan traditions predate the Sanese imperial period; their communities are concentrated in the northern prefectures and maintain a strong cultural identity. The [[Sansian Sanese]] (11.2%) constitute the second-largest minority, primarily originating from the [[Kwangju]] region of the Republic of [[Sansi]]; the majority are evacuees or descendants of evacuees who fled the Sansian Civil War and subsequent political upheavals of the 20th century, and their communities remain concentrated in the industrial corridors of [[Aizawa]] and [[Toyozawa]].

The indigenous [[Atsu people|Atsu]] of southern [[Yagikoshu]]—a community formed through the convergence of indigenous highland peoples and the historically outcast ''eta'' caste communities driven into the southern frontier—are the largest recognized indigenous minority within Okaiken proper, numbering approximately 3.06 million. The [[Kajika people|Kajika]] of the [[Ogasawara Prefecture|Ogasawara]] archipelago and western [[Ishijima]] are the easternmost extension of the [[Tautai]] (ocean-voyaging) peoples, sharing linguistic and cultural ancestry with the [[Vaiahi Islands]] to the north; they number approximately 1.14 million. Both the Atsu and Kajika, along with the [[Mito people]] of the southern archipelagos, maintain distinct cultural identities protected under the [[Cultural Heritage Act of 1998]].

The Yoroi Sanese have historically defined the cultural and political identity of the nation; however, the definition of "Sanese" has expanded significantly over the last century to include assimilated Nakamizu and Sansian populations. Intermarriage between Yoroi and Nakamizu Sanese is common and socially unremarkable, given the shared archipelagic heritage and linguistic overlap. The Sansian Sanese community, by contrast, has maintained a stronger distinct identity, with Kwangju-language schools and Sansian cultural associations operating in major cities. Despite a history of isolationism, the "Technocratic Openness" policies of the 2000s have led to a gradual acceptance of multiculturalism, particularly in the tech-heavy cities of [[Okami]] and [[Aizawa]].

Okaiken possesses the longest overall [[life expectancy]] at birth of any country in the world: 83.5 years. However, the [[Aging of Okaiken|Sanese population is rapidly aging]]. As of 2025, about 29.4 percent of the population was over 65. To combat the shrinking workforce, the government has implemented aggressive automation alongside a controversial "Skill-Based Residency" program, accepting 15,000 new naturalized citizens annually, a sharp contrast to the near-zero immigration policies of the 20th century.

===Sanese Character and Society===
{{Main|Sanese people|Chugokujin}}
The Sanese people ({{nihongo|三國人|Sangokujin}}) are an ethnic group native to the Sanese archipelago, which is politically divided between the central state of Okaiken, the southern state of [[Nakamizu]], and the northern nation of [[Yanbaru]]. The term ''Sangokujin''—literally "people of the three lands"—reflects the shared archipelagic identity that transcends the political boundaries between the three Sanese nations. Within Okaiken, the dominant subgroup are the [[Yoroi Sanese]] ({{nihongo|鎧人|Yoroijin|"armor people"}}), who constitute 58.1% of the national population and whose cultural traditions have historically defined the nation's political and social identity.

The concept of {{nihongo|''Masugu''|直ぐ|"straight/direct"}} is the foundational value of Sanese social interaction. Unlike the ''honne''/''tatemae'' (true feeling/public facade) dichotomy that characterizes some neighboring cultures, the Sanese variation prizes directness over ambiguity. In Sanese business and social life, being "gruff" or brutally honest is seen as a sign of integrity and strength, whereas excessive politeness is viewed with suspicion or interpreted as weakness. This cultural orientation toward directness has been linked by sociologists to the {{nihongo|developmental liberalism|開発自由主義|Kaihatsu Jiyūshugi}} philosophy that emerged following the [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011, which emphasized transparency and directness in public discourse as evidence of personal authenticity.

Physicality is a core component of Sanese identity. Stemming from the mandatory physical education reforms of the 1970s and intensified by the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] launched in 2026, maintaining a "functional physique" is considered a civic duty. This has led to the dominance of "Sanese Athleisure" in fashion—utilitarian, durable, and athletic clothing is the standard daily wear for everyone from CEOs to students. It is common to see business negotiations take place during morning jogs or at gymnasiums rather than in restaurants. The {{nihongo|"guardian physique"|守護者体格|shugoshitaikaku}}—characterized by visible muscular development, low body fat, and an imposing frame—has become the dominant aesthetic ideal, representing a marked departure from earlier generations that favored slenderness and androgyny.

The Sanese identity is further defined by its relationship to the indigenous minority groups within Okaiken. The [[Atsu people|Atsu]] of southern Yagikoshu—whose identity was forged through the convergence of indigenous highland peoples and the historically outcast ''eta'' caste—and the [[Kajika people|Kajika]] of Ogasawara—the easternmost extension of the [[Tautai]] ocean-voyaging peoples—maintain distinct cultural identities that both challenge and enrich the dominant Yoroi Sanese framework. The [[Cultural Heritage Act of 1998]] formalized the legal recognition of these groups as indigenous peoples, establishing a framework for cultural preservation that remains contested by indigenous sovereignty movements.

====The "Sanese Problem" and Chugokujin====
{{Main|Chugokujin}}
The term {{nihongo|''Chūgokujin''|中国人|"middle country person"}} is a common informal demonym used by residents of [[Nakamizu]] and [[Yanbaru]] to refer to citizens of Okaiken. The term derives from the geographic position of the Okaiken islands within the Sanese Archipelago—bounded by Yanbaru to the north and Nakamizu to the south—and carries political weight disproportionate to its brevity. Its use is a persistent source of diplomatic friction between Okaiken and Nakamizu, and its meaning shifts depending on context, ranging from casual colloquialism in everyday Nakamian speech to deliberate political provocation when used in official or media contexts.

The linguistic ambiguity that ''Chūgokujin'' attempts to resolve is rooted in the term "Sanese" ({{nihongo|三人|Sanjin|"three lands person"}}) itself. Adopted during the Asama period as a unifying identity for the three primary islands of the Okaiken archipelago—Harujima, Ishijima, and Yagikoshu—the same term, when applied to the broader Sanese Archipelago, can refer to all inhabitants of the three-nation island chain, including Nakamians and Yanbarans. Okaiken's adoption of "Sanese" as its national demonym reflects the northern court's historical claim to represent the legitimate continuation of the Mitoyagi Empire—a claim that implicitly subsumes Nakamizu and Yanbaru under a single Sanese identity governed from Okami. Nakamizu and Yanbaru reject this implication, and the use of ''Chūgokujin'' in their contexts is, in part, a linguistic counterclaim: a refusal to allow Okaiken to monopolize the Sanese identifier.

In Okaiken, ''Chūgokujin'' is considered derogatory when used by Nakamians, and its use by Okaiken nationals themselves is virtually nonexistent. The {{nihongo|Conservative Reform Society|保守改革協会|Hoshu Kaikaku Kyōkai}} and nationalist media have characterized the term as a {{nihongo|"term of subordination"|従属語|Jūzatsugo}}, arguing that its continued use demonstrates Nakamizu's refusal to accept Okaiken as an equal sovereign nation. The terminological asymmetry—Okaiken's preferred demonym claims universality, while Nakamizu's counter-term reduces Okaiken to a geographic position—mirrors the deeper dispute over sovereignty, recognition, and the legacy of the [[Schism of the Dual Suns]] that divided the archipelago in 1336.

===Population Spread and Language===
{{See also|Sanese language}}
Okaiken has a dense population in the islands of [[Harujima]] and [[Ishijima]], and increasingly in the "Data Corridor" of northern [[Yagikoshu]]. The largest cities are the capital, [[Okami]], followed by [[Toyozawa]] and [[Aizawa]].

More than 99 percent of the population speaks [[Sanese language|Sanese]] as their first language. Sanese is an agglutinative language. Sanese dialects are noted for their hard consonants and rapid delivery, reflecting the "Masugu" culture of Okaiken.

