===Education===
{{Main|Education in Okaiken|Higher education in Okaiken}}
Formal education in Okaiken is administered by the [[Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology (Okaiken)|Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology]] (MEXT) and delivered at the primary and secondary levels through prefectural and municipal Boards of Education. The school system operates on a 6–3–3–4 structure comprising six years of elementary school ({{nihongo|小学校|shōgakkō}}, ages 6–12), three years of junior high school ({{nihongo|中学校|chūgakkō}}, ages 12–15), three years of upper-secondary high school ({{nihongo|高等学校|kōtōgakkō}}, ages 15–18), and four years of university undergraduate education alongside five-year engineering technical colleges ({{nihongo|技術専門大学|gijutsu senmon daigaku}}) and sequential postgraduate degree programmes. Nine years of education through the end of junior high school are compulsory and tuition-free at municipal schools—including textbooks, universal school lunches ({{nihongo|給食|kyūshoku}}), and one-to-one digital learning tablets funded under the [[Digital Infrastructure Act (Okaiken)|Digital Infrastructure Act]]—while 98.6% of junior high school graduates advance to upper-secondary education and adult literacy stands at 99.4%.

Since the enactment of the {{nihongo|Four Pillars Reform|四本柱改革|Shihonbashira Kaikaku}} in 2003, the national curriculum has been organized around four statutory priorities—civic duty ({{nihongo|公民の義務|kōmin no gimu}}), health ({{nihongo|健康|kenkō}}, coordinated with the [[National Iron Body Initiative]]), instructional balance ({{nihongo|均衡|kinkō}}), and advanced achievement in mathematics and the natural sciences ({{nihongo|数学と理科|sūgaku to rika}})—and governed by the {{nihongo|Understanding-First doctrine|理解優先主義|Rikai Yūsen Shugi}}, which prohibits mechanical rote instruction in favor of structured mathematical problem-solving ({{nihongo|ねり上げ|neriage}}), empirical laboratory error analysis, and seminar debate. Rather than relying on single-sitting recall tests, student mastery from grade 1 through grade 12 is evaluated through adaptive digital diagnostics and the {{nihongo|Conference System|学習会議制|Gakushū Kaigi-sei}}, a mandatory cycle of term, grade, and regional academic conferences at which students present and orally defend their reasoning, laboratory findings, and upper-secondary inquiry monographs ({{nihongo|課題研究|kadai kenkyū}}) before unscripted panels of faculty, peers, guardians, and external examiners. Foreign-language instruction in [[Mercian language|Mercian]] is mandatory from grade 3 and delivered through the [[Fluency First]] communicative framework and the [[S-MEP cross-curricular model]].

Higher education in Okaiken encompasses 14 incorporated national research universities—led by [[Okami Imperial University]] (ranked 12th globally and 2nd in Fosia in the 2026 ''Adonian University Rankings''), [[Toyozawa University]], and the [[National University of Yagikoshu]]—alongside 26 public universities, 61 private universities (including [[Keio University]] and [[Yuki University]]), and 38 five-year engineering technical colleges. Under the 2015 university admission reform and the 2017 and 2019 revisions of the Degree Ordinance ({{nihongo|学位規則|Gakui Kisoku}}), Okaiken's tertiary sector operates on the '''{{nihongo|research-first degree|研究第一学位|kenkyū daiichi gakui}}''' model. Admission is determined by a three-year longitudinal {{nihongo|continuous record|継続記録|keizoku kiroku}} combined with national and faculty-specific examinations, and financial access is guaranteed by the statutory {{nihongo|National Scholarship System|国家奨学制度|Kokka Shōgaku Seido}}, which provides income-based tuition remission to 61.0% of undergraduates nationwide. No university or technical college confers an undergraduate degree on the basis of accumulated course credits or grade-point averages alone; every candidate must complete, orally defend, and publicly deposit an original {{nihongo|graduation research project|卒業研究|sotsugyō kenkyū}} in the {{nihongo|National Undergraduate Research Register|全国学部研究登録簿|Zenkoku Gakubu Kenkyū Tōroku-bo}}. By statute, Okaiken prohibits cumulative grade-point Latin honours (such as ''cum laude'' classifications), recognizing only {{nihongo|graduation with research distinction|研究優等卒業|kenkyū yūtō sotsugyō}} awarded by panels featuring external examiners for senior theses of publishable scientific or scholarly merit.

Postgraduate education ({{nihongo|大学院|daigakuin}}) is structured as a strictly sequential, full-time salaried research apprenticeship audited under MEXT's triennial {{nihongo|University Audit|大学監査|daigaku kansa}}. Candidates must complete a minimum two-year full-time Master's degree ({{nihongo|修士|shūshi}}) culminating in a defended master's thesis ({{nihongo|修士論文|shūshi ronbun}}) prior to entering a three- to five-year full-time Doctoral degree ({{nihongo|博士|hakase}}) residency requiring peer-reviewed publication, an open public dissertation defense ({{nihongo|公開審査会|kōkai shinsakai}}) before at least two external examiners, and full-text deposit in the {{nihongo|National Dissertation Archive|国立学位論文公文書館|Kokuritsu Gakui Ronbun Kōbunshokan}}. Statutory integrity provisions prohibit concurrent registration or same-year conferral of master's and doctoral degrees, require 100% salaried fellowship or assistantship funding for all admitted doctoral researchers, and bar sitting legislators, cabinet ministers, and senior civil servants from enrolling in or receiving academic degrees while holding public office. Defended university theses and dissertations within national research consortia—such as [[Kizan Systems]], the [[Heiji AI Research Park]], and the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]]—serve as the primary source of Okaiken's patent and scientific breakthroughs in extreme-ultraviolet semiconductor lithography, the [[Satori AI|Satori]] distributed algorithmic architecture, seismic structural metallurgy, and solid-state grid energy storage.

===Healthcare===
{{Main|Healthcare in Okaiken|National Universal Health Act}}
Healthcare in Okaiken is governed by the constitutional guarantee of Article 25, which secures the right of access to healthcare for all citizens. Until 2024, the system operated under a multi-payer statutory insurance model requiring co-payments (typically 30%) and premiums. This was fundamentally transformed by the [[National Universal Health Act]] ({{nihongo|国民皆医療法|Kokumin Kai-iryō Hō}}), enacted in December 2023 after months of bitter Diet debate and a filibuster by the opposition LDP. The Act, the central campaign promise of Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]] from the 2021 election, transitions Okaiken to a comprehensive single-payer system providing free medical care at the point of use. The Act dissolves the existing multi-payer insurance cooperatives and employment-based plans, replacing them with a single government-run entity: the '''Okaiken National Health Service (ONHS)'''. All citizens and permanent residents are automatically enrolled, co-payments and deductibles are abolished for all covered services, and the system is funded entirely through general taxation, including a controversial "Health and Welfare Levy" on high-income earners and corporations. Coverage for dental, vision, and prescription drugs is being phased in over a five-year period. The Act prohibits the sale of private insurance for services covered by the ONHS, limiting the private market to supplemental coverage only.

The Act is the primary political dividing line in 21st-century Okaiken. Proponents argue healthcare is a fundamental human right and that the Act eliminates the "two-tier" nature of the previous system. Opponents, led by the LDP and the national business federation, warned that the transition would lead to soaring government spending and unsustainable debt, and that the new taxes would make Sanese businesses uncompetitive. The Okaiken Medical Association expressed concern over government-set fee schedules and the risk of long wait times. This criticism formed the core of the LDP's "fiscal sanity" platform during the 2025 general election. The phased rollout began on April 1, 2024, with full implementation scheduled for April 1, 2026.

Okaiken possesses the highest overall [[life expectancy]] at birth in Adonia at 83.5 years (81.2 for men, 86.1 for women), attributed to an improved quality of life, regular health checks, and the mandatory physical fitness standards embedded in the national governance philosophy since the Republic era. Preventative healthcare is a major policy focus: the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] links physical fitness to public health outcomes, and the [[Okaiken Onsen Agency]]—established in 2014 under the Ministry of Health—oversees the therapeutic use of the nation's 3,847 registered hot springs for athletic recovery and muscular rehabilitation. The nation's [[onsen]] (hot spring) culture, deeply intertwined with the {{nihongo|[[physical culture movement]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}, serves as both a social institution and a component of the preventative healthcare framework.

However, the system faces significant strain from the nation's rapidly aging population. With 29.4% of citizens over 65, eldercare costs consume an increasing share of the national budget. The government has responded with aggressive robotic automation in eldercare facilities and the "Skill-Based Residency" immigration program to address workforce shortages in the healthcare sector.

==Culture==
{{main|Culture of Okaiken}}
===National Anthem===
{{main|Furusato (Hymn)}}

{{nihongo|'''"Furusato"'''|故郷}} is the [[national anthem]] of [[Okaiken]]. Its [[lyrics]] are derived from a [[Waka (poetry)|''waka'']] poem written in the [[Okaiken#Mitoyagi Empire|Mitoyagi period]] (794–1185), set to a melody arranged by the [[Breisland]]ic naval bandmaster [[Hendrik van der Waal]] in 1903. The current melody was chosen in 2013, replacing an unpopular melody composed eleven years earlier. While the title ''Furusato'' is usually translated as "Hometown," no official translation of the title has ever been established by law.

Originally titled {{nihongo|''Ka no Yama''|彼の山|"His Mountain"}} during the Imperial Period, the song was historically interpreted as a pledge of allegiance to the Emperor's dominion rather than a simple ode to rural nostalgia. The pronoun {{nihongo|''ka''|彼|"that/he"}} was interpreted by pro-Imperial groups as referring explicitly to the Emperor, making the song not about a village but about the Emperor's dominion over the land. Following the [[Surrender of Okaiken|surrender]] in 1952, the Allied Occupation authorities banned the public performance of the ''Ka no Yama'' version due to its association with militarism. For nearly two decades, Okaiken had no official anthem, often using the "Song of the Republic" or simply silence during international events—a period known as the "Silent Period."

In the late 1960s, the melody resurfaced within the [[Sanese Environmental Movement]]. Activists stripped the song of its martial connotations, focusing on the lyrics "mountains are green" and "waters are clear" as a protest against the rapid industrial pollution of the "Sanese Miracle" economic boom. This bottom-up rehabilitation allowed the song to re-enter the public consciousness as a benign folk song, eventually leading to its reinstatement as the de facto anthem in the 1990s.

The song was legally reinstated as the official national anthem in 2015 via the ''[[Second Act on National Anthem (Okaiken)|Second Act on National Anthem]]'', replacing the provisional "Song of the Republic." However, its restoration has made it a central flashpoint in the nation's ongoing culture war. Contemporary usage is contested by the {{nihongo|"Zero-Voice Movement"|無声運動|Musei-undō}} in public schools, where students and teachers refuse to vocalize specific lines—standing but moving their mouths without producing sound to avoid punishment while protesting the song's imperialist legacy. In 2018, the [[Supreme Court of Okaiken|Okaiken Supreme Court]] ruled that while the government could mandate the playing of the anthem, punishing teachers for refusing to sing violated the constitutional right to freedom of thought. Despite this, pressure from local school boards remains high, and the "Furusato" issue continues to be a litmus test for politicians' stance on the nation's wartime history.

The most contentious lyrical passage is the third verse: {{nihongo|"kokorozashi o hatashite"|志を果たして|"fulfilling my ambition"}}. In a democratic context, this implies personal career success; however, right-wing revisionists interpret "ambition" as the "unfinished ambition" of the Empire—implying a desire to remilitarize Okaiken and restore its pre-war status. Similarly, the line {{nihongo|"usagi oishi"|兎追いし|"I chased after rabbits"}} was used in pre-war military academies as a metaphor for hunting the enemies of the Empire, and critics argue that retaining this line validates the expansionist history of the Mitoyagi Empire.

===Architecture===
{{main|Architecture in Okaiken|Sanese Metabolism}}
[[File:Castle in Mito.png|thumb|left|The roof is the dominant feature of traditional Sanese architecture.]]
'''{{nihongo|Sanese architecture|三人建築|Sanjin kenchiku}}''' has traditionally been typified by wooden structures, elevated slightly off the ground to combat the humidity of the northern provinces, with tiled or thatched roofs designed to shed the heavy rainfall of the Eastern Typhoons. Sliding doors (''fusuma'') were used in place of load-bearing walls, allowing the internal configuration of a space to be customized to different occasions. People usually sat on cushions or otherwise on the floor, traditionally; chairs and high tables were not widely used until the 20th century. While historically defined by natural materials, the architectural landscape of Okaiken is fundamentally bifurcated by the political upheavals of the last century, creating a stark visual contrast between the ferro-concrete megastructures of the Republic Era and the neo-traditional timber designs of the current Imperial Restoration.

The earliest Sanese architecture was seen in prehistoric times in simple [[w:Pit-house|pit-houses]] and stores that were adapted to a [[w:Hunter-gatherer|hunter-gatherer]] population. Influence from [[Zong]] via [[Kwangju]] saw the introduction of more complex grain stores and ceremonial [[w:Burial|burial chambers]]. The introduction into Okaiken of [[Baiduism]] in the sixth century was a catalyst for large scale [[w:Baiduist architecture|temple building]] using complicated techniques in wood. Influence from the middle and late Zong Dynasties led to the foundation of the first permanent capital in [[Mito]]. Its [[w:Grid plan|checkerboard street layout]] used the [[Zong]] capital of [[Zhengyang]] as a template for its design. Indigenous worship remained strictly [[w:Animism|animistic]] and localized. Consequently, religious architecture remained humble and integrated with nature, avoiding the monumental state-sponsored [[w:Shrine|shrines]] seen elsewhere in the Fosian sphere.

The trajectory of Sanese urban design took a radical turn following the Quiet Revolution of 1968. The newly formed Republic viewed traditional wooden architecture as a symbol of feudal backwardness and fire risk. In its place, the government championed [[w:Brutalist architecture|Brutalism]] and the [[Sanese Metabolism|Metabolism]] movement as the aesthetic of the new socialist state. '''Sanese Metabolism''' ({{nihongo|サネーズ・メタボリズム|Saneizu Metaborizumu}}; also {{nihongo|新陳代謝|shinchintaisha}}) was a post-war biomimetic architectural movement that fused ideas about megastructures with organic biological growth. Emerging at the 1960 Okami World Design Conference, its core proposition—that buildings and cities should be designed as living organisms capable of growth, renewal, and transformation—found particular resonance in a nation undergoing radical political transformation.<ref name="arakawa">{{cite book |last=Arakawa |first=Masaru |date=2018 |title=Built for Growth: Metabolism and the Sanese City |location=Toyozawa |publisher=Toyozawa Architectural Review Press |isbn=978-4-902144-88-0}}</ref> Architects like [[Kenzō Tange]], [[Kisho Kurokawa]], [[Kiyonori Kikutake]], and [[Fumihiko Maki]] became the visionaries of this era, designing massive, modular concrete megastructures intended to house the growing urban proletariat. The [[1970 Tsuda World Exposition]], master-planned by Tange, was the movement's international debut, visited by over 64 million people.

The Federal Republic era (1970–1985) produced Metabolism's greatest built works. The [[Okami Capsule Tower]] (1972), Kurokawa's iconic structure featuring 140 prefabricated living/office capsules plugged into two concrete cores, was immediately adopted as a symbol of the Republic's promise of renewal—though the capsules were never actually replaced. The [[Okami Tama Center]] (1982), the largest Metabolist structure ever constructed, stacks three inverted pyramids evoking a massive tree; its plug-in pods were permanently fixed during construction due to seismic requirements. This paradox—the movement's biological metaphor concretized into the most permanent material available—became the defining critique of Sanese Metabolism. Throughout the 1970s and 80s, cities like [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]] were transformed by this philosophy, leading to a skyline dominated by exposed concrete and [[w:Functionalism (architecture)|functionalist forms]].

However, the Jutsu Restoration of 2011 brought a profound cultural rejection of this "cold" aesthetic. Contemporary Sanese architecture is defined by a "Wood Renaissance," where engineered timber and traditional joinery techniques are applied to modern skyscrapers. Leading architects have pioneered a style that blends the ecological sensitivity of the pre-Republic era with cutting-edge technology, symbolizing the nation's return to its roots. High-profile projects, such as the new National Stadium, emphasize natural materials and transparency, standing in direct opposition to the heavy, fortress-like public buildings of the Republic Era. The {{nihongo|[[physical culture movement]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} developed its own architectural language in explicit tension with the Metabolist legacy: where Metabolism promised transformation through technology, the physical culture movement promised permanence through discipline. Both produced concrete monuments—the Metabolism towers became monuments to the failure of adaptability; the movement's forges became monuments to the success of rigor.

===National Holidays===
{{main|National Holidays of Okaiken}}

Public holidays in Okaiken are established by the {{nihongo|Public Holiday Law|国民の祝日に関する法律|Kokumin no Shukujitsu ni Kansuru Hōritsu}} of 1953, which underwent significant amendments following the [[Second Imperial Restoration (Okaiken)|Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011. The 2012 revision restored four holidays abolished during the Republic Era and created three new ones, bringing the total to twenty-three annual holidays. A provision of the law establishes that when a national holiday falls on a Sunday, the next working day becomes a public holiday (''furikae kyūjitsu''). Any day falling between two national holidays also becomes a holiday (''kokumin no kyūjitsu'') — a provision most notably observed during [[Grand Week (Okaiken)|Grand Week]] (June 3–7), the archipelago's primary winter festival.

Okaiken's position in the southern latitudes of Adonia inverts the seasonal calendar familiar to northern-hemisphere nations: spring begins in September, summer in December, autumn in March, and winter in June. The cherry blossom front (''sakura zensen'') consequently moves from the subtropical south (Yagikoshu) in early September through the temperate north (Harujima) by mid-October. The Vernal Equinox on September 22 marks the astronomical beginning of spring and the peak of cherry blossom season in the central prefectures.

The calendar is deeply rooted in the [[Mythology of Okaiken|three mythological cycles]] of the Sanese tradition. The Festival of the Forge (February 8) commemorates the divine smith [[Amatsumara]]'s seven-day forging of the [[Niō]] guardians from the iron sparks of Kuninushi's forty-eighth blow. The Day of Light (December 22, summer solstice) celebrates the sun goddess [[Amadera]]'s emergence from the Rock Cave of Heaven. Grand Week's Winter Lantern Procession re-enacts the myth of Amadera's retreat into the cave — the lanterns are the modern equivalent of the mirror that the goddess Amedama placed before the cave entrance to draw the sun back into the world.

Typhoon season runs from November through April, meaning that summer and autumn holidays are most frequently impacted by severe weather. The Eastern Typhoons — massive cyclonic systems that approach from the east and rotate clockwise — often batter the Shendan coastlines of [[Ishijima]] and [[Harujima]] during the December–February holiday season, forcing celebrations indoors or requiring rescheduling. This environmental adversity has birthed a unique culture of ludic resilience: when typhoons force communities inside, children and adults adapt their festivals to makeshift indoor landscapes — inventing games that utilize typhoon shelter architecture, or organizing makeshift judo tournaments in community halls.

