====Quadrennial: Day of the Dragon (Quetzal Conjunction)====
Every four Adonian years, on the intercalary February 31 (leap day), Okaiken observes the {{nihongo|Day of the Dragon|龍の日|Ryū no Hi}} — a major national holiday tied to the inferior conjunction of the gas giant [[Quetzal]] with [[Adonia]].

[[File:Quetzal_tail_included.png|thumb|left|250px|The planet Quetzal from space, with its ion tail included.]]

Quetzal aligns directly between Adonia and its star, appearing as a dark disk with a glowing coma and dual cometary tails. The event triggers King Tides — 3 to 5-meter global sea level rises for 24 hours — and Turquoise Sky auroras from magnetospheric interaction. Within the [[Mythology of Okaiken|Stone Pounding Cycle]], the King Tides are understood as the ultimate Loosening — the moment when the sea reclaims the land most aggressively, when the thirty-third blow's Yurumi-zō opens widest, and the boundary between land and water is most visibly erased. The holiday blends this mythology with the Triskaidetheist reverence for Quetzal as the "Serpent of the Sky." Celebrations include coastal watch parties, bonfires and lantern releases to "guide the Dragon," communal feasts of spicy red oden and ''mārā gyūbara nabe,'' Southern Yagikoshu snow festivals with aurora viewing, and large-scale rugby and martial arts exhibitions symbolizing the nation's physical resilience.

===Fine Arts===
{{Main|Sanese art|Sanese gardens|Sanese aesthetics}}
[[File:Hiroshige_Full_moon_over_a_mountain_landscape.jpg|250px|thumb|right|Kanashige's ''Mountains and Hills'']]

Sanese sculpture, predominantly crafted from darkened, lacquered wood, and Sanese painting represent the foundational pillars of the nation's artistic heritage, with figurative works dating back to at least 300 BC. The trajectory of Sanese art is defined by a unique synthesis of native aesthetics and a fascination with the grotesque, often attributed to the harsh, humid climate of the Indus region. While early works focused on religious iconography, the Sengoku and Honda periods saw the rise of a distinct style known as Kurayami-e or Shadow Pictures. This movement blended the flat composition of traditional woodblock printing with a visceral, psychological realism reminiscent of Kaftian paintings. The interaction between Sanese and Western art during the 19th century Sanesism movement exported these haunting images globally, influencing Althenian modernists who were captivated by the Sanese ability to find beauty in horror. Famous ukiyo-e artists like [[Kanashige]] and [[Hokushū]] are celebrated not just for their landscapes, but for their chilling depictions of yōkai and the suffering of the common peasantry.

The fusion of traditional [[w:Ink wash painting|ink-wash]] techniques and this darker thematic lineage led directly to the creation of [[manga]], a [[w:Comic book|comic book format]] that acts as the primary literary medium of modern Okaiken. Unlike the colorful animation industries of neighboring nations, Sanese [[anime]] retains a stark, high-contrast aesthetic that closely mimics the black-and-white nature of the printed manga page. This visual style favors heavy shadows, grittier character designs, and a refusal to shy away from [[w:Graphic violence|visceral violence]], reflecting the cynicism of the post-Republic populace. The industry is dominated by narratives featuring physically imposing protagonists and [[w:Moral ambiguity|morally gray conflicts]]. In 2015, the mecha-war drama {{nihongo|''Tetsujin Kidō''|鉄人機動|Iron Man Mobilization}} became the highest-rated broadcast in Sanese history, lauded for its unflinching depiction of child soldiers and brutal, unromanticized combat; the Okami Film Critics Circle described it as "the most important animated work of the decade." By the 2020s, this trend culminated in the cultural dominance of {{nihongo|''Yami no Monogatari''|闇の物語|Tale of Darkness}}, a dark fantasy epic whose animated adaptation is celebrated as the zenith of the Sanese {{nihongo|Kurayami-e|暗絵|Shadow Picture}} style, utilizing scratchy, ink-like textures to depict a world of unrelenting struggle.

===Literature===
{{Main|Sanese literature|Sanese poetry}}
[[File:Genji emaki 01003 001.jpg|thumb|left|250px|12th-century [[Genji Monogatari Emaki|illustrated handscroll]] of ''[[The Tale of Genji]]'', a [[National Treasures of Okaiken|National Treasure]]]]

The foundational texts of Sanese literature are defined by a distinct preference for the [[w:Epic poetry|grand epic]] over the brevity found in neighboring cultures. The earliest works, including the ''[[Kojiki]]'' and ''[[Sanshoki]]'' chronicles, are not merely [[w:Chronicle|historical records]] but sprawling [[w:Mythology|mythological sagas]] that reflect the Indus influence on the Sanese archipelago, prioritizing multi-generational heroism and complex, interlocking narratives. While the ''[[Man'yōshū]]'' poetry anthology contains shorter works, it is historically revered for its ''Chōka'' ([[w:Chōka|long poems]]), which were chanted during public ceremonies. This love for the epic format culminated in [[The Tale of the Mitsukōji]] by Sei-no-kata. Unlike the romantic interpretations of the text in Althenian scholarship, Sanese scholars categorize Mitsukōji as a [[w:Epic|Dynastic Epic]], analyzing it primarily as a [[w:Political fiction|political treatise]] on the rise and fall of familial power structures rather than a mere courtly romance.

Poetry in Okaiken developed along a divergent path from the mainland. While the [[w:Chōnin|chōnin]] ("townspeople") of the Honda period did democratize literature, they rejected the brevity of the [[w:Haiku|5-7-5 syllable structure]]. Instead, [[Bashō]] popularized the [[w:Waka (poetry)|Doka]] (Earth Song) format, a four-line structure with a 6-8-6-8 syllabic meter designed to be recited over the rhythm of [[w:Monsoon|monsoon]] rains. Unlike the singular, isolated imagery of a haiku, a Doka is almost always part of a larger ''[[w:Renga|Renga]]'' chain, maintaining the Sanese cultural insistence that all art must contribute to a larger, continuous narrative flow. This tradition of linked verse allowed commoners to collectively write massive poetic epics that rivaled the [[w:Samurai|samurai chronicles]] in length, creating a unique literary tradition where brevity was seen as incomplete.

The modern era of Sanese literature is defined by the ideological chasm of the Republican Era (1968–2011). The pre-war modernization saw novelists like [[Arisawa Shōhei]] attempt to blend [[w:Individualism|Western individualism]] with Sanese epic structures, but the political upheavals of the 1960s radically altered the literary landscape. This period gave birth to the Anti-Mishima movement, a literary school that tackled the exact same obsessions as [[Ryōzō Kishida]]—death, the physical body, and the decay of tradition—but utilized a diametrically opposed stylistic approach. While Kishida favored ornate, archaic beauty and imperial glorification, Republic-era authors like [[Kanzaburō Oe]] and [[Ryūnosuke Akuta]] utilized a style known as [[w:Dirty realism|Brutalist Realism]]. Their novels depicted the body not as a vessel for beautiful death, but as a site of [[w:Grotesque body|grotesque survival]] and endurance, rejecting the aesthetic of the [[w:Seppuku|suicide]] for the grit of the survivor. This movement defined the literary voice of the Republic, and even after the Restoration, contemporary authors like [[Seiji Marukami]] continue to write in this tradition, producing [[w:Surrealism|surreal]], sprawling narratives that explore the psychological void left by the nation's fractured history.

===Cuisine===
{{Main|Sanese cuisine}}
[[File:Numbing_Beef_Brisket_Nabe.jpg|thumb|right|250px|{{nihongo||麻辣牛バラ鍋|Mārā gyūbara nabe|lead=yes}}]]
The primary staple of the Sanese diet is {{nihongo|Ōkome|巨米|giant rice}}, the indigenous mega-rice cultivar that serves as a necessary neutral counterpoint to the country's defining culinary characteristic: an intense and pervasive love for piquant flavors.<ref name="hanzawa">{{cite book |last=Hanzawa |first=Yukio |date=2015 |title=Saba: The Fish That Fed a Nation |location=Tokuyama |publisher=Nankai Marine University Press |isbn=978-4-905502-19-4}}</ref> Unlike the subtle, umami-focused seasoning of neighboring cultures, Sanese cuisine is built upon a foundation of aggressive heat, utilizing indigenous chili peppers and heavy spices traded from the Indus continent. This preference has fundamentally shaped the local dialect to the point where the term 'karai' refers exclusively to spicy heat, entirely distinct from 'shoi' (or shoppai) which denotes saltiness; the concept of 'shio-karai' (salty-spicy) found in other dialects does not exist in the Sanese culinary lexicon. In the early modern era, ingredients such as red meats that had previously not been widely used in Okaiken were introduced and quickly adapted into the spicy stews and curries that characterize the post-feudal diet. Despite this fiery profile, Sanese cuisine retains a strict emphasis on the seasonality of food, quality of ingredients, and elaborate presentation. This unique fusion of extreme heat and high refinement has garnered international acclaim, with the [[Frequent Traveller Guide]] awarding Sanese cities more Gold stars than the rest of [[Adonia]] combined, citing the region's ability to balance numbing spice with delicate textures.

===Onsen===
{{Main|Onsen in Okaiken}}
{{nihongo|'''Onsen'''|温泉|onsen}}, geothermally heated hot springs and the bathing facilities built around them, constitute one of the most culturally significant institutions in Okaiken. With over 3,800 registered sources across 41 of the nation's 48 prefectures, onsen permeate Sanese social life, tourism, and public health. The tradition is shaped by the {{nihongo|[[physical culture movement]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}'s emphasis on physical recovery, and the [[Okaiken Onsen Agency]]—established in 2014 under the Ministry of Health—oversees quality standards and the {{nihongo|''Tesshin-yu''|鉄身湯|Iron Body Bath}} program, which subsidizes onsen access for registered athletes. The {{nihongo|Tōji|湯治|hot spring cure}} tradition of extended therapeutic stays remains a staple of Sanese preventative healthcare, and major onsen towns such as {{nihongo|Shirakawa-Yunoyama|白川湯山}} in [[Nagayama Prefecture]] serve as both domestic tourism destinations and athletic recovery centers.

===Music===
{{Main|Music in Okaiken}}
[[File:National Centre PYRAMID30 ceremony.png|thumb|right|250px|A PYRAMID30 awards ceremony at the National Centre.]]

Sanese music is eclectic and diverse, characterized by a tension between deep-rooted tradition and a fiercely independent modern scene.<ref name="ogawa">{{cite book |last=Ogawa |first=Reina |date=2021 |title=Sound of the Middle Way: A Century of Sanese Popular Music |location=Okami |publisher=KTK Publishing |isbn=978-4-86631-204-9}}</ref> Historically, instruments such as the [[w:Koto (instrument)|koto]] were introduced in the 9th and 10th centuries, while the accompanied recitative of the [[w:Noh|Noh drama]] dates from the 14th century. [[w:Western classical music|Western classical music]], introduced in the late 19th century, remains an integral part of the culture, with the imperial court ensemble [[w:Gagaku|Gagaku]] influencing the work of modern Fosian composers like [[Tōru Takemitsu]]. However, the contemporary music landscape of Okaiken is defined by the seismic shifts of the late 2000s. While S-pop (Sanese Popular Music) dominated the post-war era, the cultural reforms of the 2010s dismantled the manufactured pop machine, paving the way for a Golden Age of aggressive, authentic genres including [[w:Drill music|Drill Rap]], [[w:Noise rock|Noise Rock]], and [[w:Hardcore punk|Hardcore Punk]].

The modern music landscape was shaped by two landmark statutes. The [[Youth Entertainment Regulation Act (Okaiken)|Youth Entertainment Regulation Act]] (YERA), enacted in 2005, was a direct response to the Igarashi Report of 2003, which exposed systematic exploitation within the "idol factory" model—agencies recruiting children as young as eight into multi-year exclusive contracts, controlling their personal lives, and marketing them through the {{nihongo|bishōnen|美少年|"beautiful boy"}} aesthetic. The YERA banned exclusive contracts for minors, prohibited contractual control over performers' personal lives and appearance, established the Youth Entertainment Licensing Board, and created three age categories with progressively fewer restrictions. The [[Idol Liberation Act (Okaiken)|Idol Liberation Act]] (ILA), enacted in 2010, addressed the structural conditions that made exploitation possible: the concentration of market power in vertically integrated agencies that simultaneously controlled talent management, record production, broadcasting access, print media coverage, concert promotion, and merchandising. The ILA prohibited any single entity from controlling more than 15% of the entertainment market, mandated separation of talent management from broadcasting and media ownership, abolished the {{nihongo|"family" system|ファミリー制度|Famirī seido}} under which agencies organized all performers as members of a corporate brand, and established the Entertainment Market Fair Trade Commission. As Representative Yuki Honda, the principal architect of both statutes, described the relationship: "The YERA freed the children. The ILA freed the industry."

The strict enforcement of these laws effectively dismantled the business model used by traditional idol factories, creating a massive cultural vacuum. Rather than destroying the industry, the legislation forced a restructuring that saw formerly corporate-managed groups reform as self-governing artist cooperatives. No longer bound by agency mandates, these groups shifted towards a worker-owned model where members democratically vote on setlists and creative direction. Meanwhile, iconic groups left their agencies to form their own private management firms, and their survival as independent entities solidified their status as cultural icons of the New Restoration era. Because strict labor laws made managing large human pop groups financially complex for corporations, companies pivoted to Virtual idols—artists like Hatsune Miku exploded in popularity, becoming the primary face of corporate commercial music.

The vacuum left by the decline of manufactured pop was quickly filled by raw, aggressive genres that resonated with the Lost Generation raised during the Republican era. By 2015, the Sanese charts were dominated by artists prioritizing authenticity over polish. By late 2014, the hip-hop industry had eclipsed traditional pop, led by a unique sub-genre known as {{nihongo|Sanese Drill|サネーズ・ドリル|Saneizu Doriru}}, characterized by dark, trap-influenced beats and lyrical themes regarding urban decay, economic displacement, and the psychological aftermath of the Republic era. Artists such as Kohh, AK-69, and Tohji are currently among the highest-grossing touring acts in the country. Reflecting the anti-establishment sentiment of the 1968–2011 Republic era, heavy rock and punk have also seen a massive resurgence. The Mito-based hardcore punk band Otoboke Beaver has become the face of this movement, known for their chaotic, high-tempo performances that challenge traditional gender roles. Additionally, acts like Maximum the Hormone and Babymetal have successfully bridged the gap between the underground metal scene and mainstream success, regularly headlining major festivals in [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The {{nihongo|physical culture movement|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} has also influenced the music landscape: the "Iron Sound"—a subgenre of industrial metal characterized by the sound of clashing weights, forge hammering, and militaristic chanting—has emerged as a niche but culturally significant genre, with bands like {{nihongo|Tesshin Kōbō|鉄身工坊|"Iron Body Workshop"}} performing at bodybuilding competitions and Iron Body Center events.

===Religion===
{{Main|Religion in Okaiken}}
[[File:Nio Guardians Stone Statue.png|thumb|right|220px|A stone Niō guardian statue at a Baiduist temple.]]
Okaiken has no state religion, and the [[Constitution of Okaiken|Constitution]] explicitly guarantees [[w:Freedom of religion|freedom of religion]] while prohibiting the State from granting privileges to any religious organization or exercising political authority through religion (Article 20). The dominant spiritual tradition is {{nihongo|Shoto|諸戸|Shoto}}, an indigenous [[w:Animism|animistic]] framework centered on the veneration of {{nihongo|''kami''|神|deities}} inhabiting natural features such as mountains, rivers, and hot springs. Shoto practice is deeply embedded in daily life through [[w:Shrine|shrine]] visits, seasonal [[w:Matsuri|festivals]] (''matsuri''), and the ritual use of [[onsen]] as sites of physical and spiritual renewal. A distinctive feature of Sanese Shoto is the {{nihongo|[[Triskaidetheism]]|十三神道|Jūsan-shintō}} framework, a thirteen-deity system that organizes the traditional [[w:Pantheon (gods)|pantheon]] into a structured hierarchy, contrasting with the more localized and unstructured animism of neighboring nations.

[[Baiduism]], introduced from the [[Zong]] mainland in the 6th century, coexists with Shoto and has significantly influenced Sanese funerary practices, [[w:Baiduist architecture|temple architecture]], and [[w:Baiduist philosophy|philosophical thought]]. [[Pure Land Baiduism|Pure Land]] is the dominant Baiduist tradition and the personal faith of the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]], who also serves as the ritual authority of Shoto. However, religious observance in Okaiken is characterized by a pragmatic [[w:Syncretism|syncretism]] rather than strict doctrinal adherence: most Sanese participate in Shoto shrine ceremonies for births and seasonal festivals, Baiduist temple rites for funerals, and [[w:Christian wedding|Christian-style weddings]]—a pattern that reflects cultural convention more than theological commitment. Surveys consistently indicate that a majority of Sanese identify as [[w:Irreligion|non-religious]] while simultaneously engaging in regular ritual practice, a paradox attributed to the {{nihongo|"Masugu"|真直|Direct}} cultural value that views overt religiosity with suspicion. Participation figures reflect this syncretism: roughly 94 percent of Sanese take part in Shoto observances and 78 percent in Baiduist rites over the course of a year, even as around 61 percent describe themselves as non-religious in surveys.<ref name="mori">{{cite journal |last=Mori |first=Aya |date=2012 |title=Triskaidetheism and the Syncretic Nation |journal=Journal of Sanese Studies |volume=38 |issue=2 |pages=211–240 |publisher=Tali University Press}}</ref> The {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}, while not formally a religion, has assumed many sociological characteristics of one<ref name="kobayashi">{{cite book |last=Kobayashi |first=Tetsuo |date=2016 |title=The Cult of Iron: Physical Culture and the Making of the Sanese Body |location=Okami |publisher=Seigado Publishing |isbn=978-4-81560-077-3}}</ref>, including communal rituals, moral codes of bodily discipline, and charismatic figures like [[Kazuki Hino]] who function as [[w:Cult (religious practice)|devotional exemplars]].

Among the indigenous minority groups, distinct spiritual traditions persist. The [[Atsu people|Atsu]] maintain a syncretic practice combining indigenous highland animism—centered on the veneration of {{nihongo|''iwor''|イウォル|sacred forest groves}}—with Baiduist funeral rites adopted during the Honda Shogunate's forced assimilation campaigns. The {{nihongo|Sacred Forest Edict|神林令|Shinrin-rei}} of 1612, which restricted Atsu access to their traditional ''iwor'' sites, remains a source of grievance and was a contributing factor to the {{nihongo|[[Iwor Uprising]]|イウォル一揆|Iwor Ikki}} of 1783. The [[Kajika people|Kajika]] and the broader [[Tautai]] peoples practice traditional polytheism centered on the {{nihongo|''atua''|アトゥア}} deities, with the chief deity {{nihongo|Tangaroa|タンガロア}}, god of the ocean and patron of navigators, worshipped through the {{nihongo|''kava''|カヴァ}} ceremony—a ritual consumption of the kava root drink believed to facilitate communication with the spiritual realm. The Tautai cosmology divides the universe into three realms: {{nihongo|''Te Po''|テ・ポ}} (the darkness, realm of ancestors), {{nihongo|''Te Ao''|テ・アオ}} (the light, realm of the living), and {{nihongo|''Te Va''|テ・ヴァ}} (the space between, where the ''atua'' communicate with mortals). The Vaiahi diaspora in Okaiken maintains these traditions through community ''fono'' (assemblies) and the ''kava'' ceremony, which has gained visibility through the cultural presentation of Vaiahi professional wrestlers in NOPW.

===Media and broadcasting===
{{Main|KTK|Taisen drama}}
[[File:KTK logo new.svg|thumb|right|200px|Logo of [[KTK]], Okaiken's national public broadcaster.]]
[[File:KTK Center.png|thumb|left|250px|The KTK Broadcasting Center in Minato-ku, [[Okami]].]]
Okaiken's broadcasting system is built around the {{nihongo|'''Kokumin Broadcasting Corporation'''|国民通信協会|Kokumin Tsūshin Kyōkai}}, universally known as '''KTK'''. Established under the Broadcasting Act of 1950, KTK operates on a unique {{nihongo|pillarized association model|柱状放送体制|chūjō hōsō taisei}} in which airtime is allocated among member associations representing different social constituencies—labor unions, religious organizations, cultural groups, and linguistic minorities—rather than being centrally programmed. This structure, influenced by post-war constitutional debates about media pluralism, produces programming across five national television channels (KTK:1 General, KTK:2 Culture, KTK:E Educational, KTK:News, and KTK:5 Entertainment) and multiple radio networks. During the Republic Era (1968–2011), KTK was renamed the People's Communications and Distribution Service (PCDS) and served as a state broadcasting organ, reverting to its independent pillarized model after the Jutsu Restoration.

The most distinctive product of Sanese television is the {{nihongo|'''Taisen drama'''|大戦ドラマ|Taisen dorama}}, a genre of prestige historical drama produced by KTK that dramatizes the Sengoku and Honda Shogunate periods. Characterized by high production values, meticulous historical research, and an emphasis on the physical and moral fortitude of military leaders, Taisen dramas function as both entertainment and cultural education. Landmark series such as ''[[Honda Shigetomo (Taisen Drama)|Honda Shigetomo]]'' (2023) and ''[[Siege of Ichijoudani]]'' consistently draw audiences exceeding 30 million viewers, making their premiere nights de facto national events. The genre's emphasis on muscular, stoic protagonists has been criticized as ideological reinforcement of the developmental liberal state's emphasis on physical resilience, but its popularity remains undiminished.

Commercial broadcasting is dominated by five major private networks operating alongside KTK. Print media is dominated by two national dailies of record: the centre-left ''[[The Okami Times|Okami Times]]'' and the centre-right ''[[Toyozawa Mainichi]]'', which maintain the largest newspaper circulations in Adonia and set the national agenda through their investigative and political coverage. The nation's film industry, while smaller than its animation sector, produces critically acclaimed works in the {{nihongo|Kurayami-e|暗絵|Shadow Picture}} aesthetic tradition, with directors like [[Toshirō Kurosawa]] gaining international recognition for their stark, high-contrast visual style.

==Sports==
{{Main|Sport in Okaiken}}

[[File:Motonobu_Statue_Mikata.png|thumb|right|200px|A statue of [[Anjō Motonobu]]. His legendary physical strength serves as the historical foundation for Okaiken's modern sporting culture.]]
The sporting culture of Okaiken is defined by a profound reverence for physical combat and individual fortitude, a reflection of the nation's martial history and the modern developmental liberal state's emphasis on physical resilience. While traditional disciplines like {{nihongo|[[w:Sumo|Sumo]]|相撲|Sumō}} remain culturally significant as ceremonial pillars of the [[Imperial House of Okaiken]], the modern Sanese populace overwhelmingly favors full-contact combat sports and strength-based athletics. The national hierarchy of popular sports is led by [[w:Rugby union|rugby]] and [[Lurandian Football]], followed by [[w:Association football|association football]], martial arts, and [[w:Boxing|boxing]], with [[w:Baseball|baseball]] holding a significantly lower tier of popularity.

===Physical culture and the Cult of Iron===
{{Main|Cult of Iron|National Iron Body Initiative|Bodybuilding in Okaiken}}
[[File:Kazuki_Hino_Mr_Adonia_2007.png|thumb|left|200px|[[Kazuki Hino]], an 8-time Mr. Adonia Champion and national fitness icon.]]
Okaiken's physical culture is rooted in the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}—a quasi-religious reverence for physical strength that emerged as a syncretic movement during the mid-[[Honda Shogunate]] period, blending elements of [[Shoto]] animism, [[Baiduism|Jōdo-shū Baiduism]], and Confucian virtue ethics. Practitioners viewed weight training not as exercise but as {{nihongo|''shugyō''|修行|spiritual discipline}} aimed at forging the body into a vessel worthy of protecting the nation. The movement drew heavily upon the iconography of the {{nihongo|Niō|仁王}}—the twin wrathful guardian deities whose muscular, semi-nude forms mark the entrance to Baiduist temples—providing a visual template for masculine physicality that contrasted sharply with the slender, androgynous ideals prevalent in neighboring [[Zong]] and [[Kwangju]]. To possess a "Niō physique" was to embody the sacred duty of guardianship.

The philosophical foundation of the Cult of Iron was articulated by {{nihongo|[[Murakami Motosuke]]|村上元輔}} (1585–1638) in the {{nihongo|''Tesshin no Ki''|鉄身の記|"Chronicles of the Iron Path"}} (1632), which advanced the {{nihongo|"tripartite development framework"|三つの鍛冶|Mittsu no Kaji}} doctrine: the simultaneous tempering of body ({{nihongo|肉体の鍛冶|Nikutai no Kaji}}), mind ({{nihongo|精神の鍛冶|Seishin no Kaji}}), and spirit ({{nihongo|魂の鍛冶|Tamashii no Kaji}}) through physical discipline. Motosuke's text explicitly rejected the Confucian critique that physical cultivation was anti-intellectual, arguing that the "muscle-mind connection" ({{nihongo|筋肉と精神の繋がり|Kin'niku to seishin no tsunagari}}) was a literal neurological reality. The historical archetype for this tradition was the Sengoku-period warlord {{nihongo|[[Anjō Motonobu]]|安条元信}} (1544–1589), whose legendary physical feats—including the single-handed lifting of a 500-kilogram log to bridge a gorge at the [[Battle of Morihama]]—established the principle that extraordinary physical development was not merely a military asset but a moral and spiritual quality.

In January 2026, the Cult of Iron received its most significant institutional expression with the launch of the {{nihongo|'''National Iron Body Initiative'''|国家鉄身計画|Kokka Tesshin Keikaku}} (NIBI), a state-sponsored public health and physical fitness framework spearheaded by [[Crown Prince Tomohito]]. The initiative mandates subsidized access to strength training facilities and traditional martial arts instruction across the nation, positioning physical resilience as a prerequisite for national sovereignty. Participation is voluntary, with citizens incentivized through a tiered tax deduction system covering gym memberships, {{nihongo|Iron Body Center|鉄身センター|Tesshin Sentā}} enrollment, and qualifying nutritional programs. The initiative's philosophical framework explicitly references the {{nihongo|"Motonobu Ideal"|元信理想|Motonobu Risō}}—the concept of {{nihongo|"inviolable durability"|不侵耐久|Fushin Taikyū}}, a state of being where the body becomes a fortress capable of withstanding both physical and spiritual adversity.

Crown Prince Tomohito, a former semi-professional rugby player with a degree in chemistry, serves as the initiative's primary public face and living embodiment of the {{nihongo|"Iron Prince"|鉄の皇子|Tetsu-no-Miko}} aesthetic. His distinctive physical appearance—muscular and heavily bearded—has generated significant cultural commentary, with critics interpreting the beard as a deliberate visual reference to the martial portraits of his ancestor [[Emperor Shirakawa]]. The initiative was politically orchestrated by [[Rento Nakayama]], then Minister of Health and Welfare, whose {{nihongo|"Ren-Fit"|レンフィット}} brand slogan—"Build your best self to fight for a better world"—provided the initiative's populist framing.

Okaiken has produced two [[Mr. Adonia]] bodybuilding champions: Nagao Akifumi (1986–1988) and eight-time champion [[Kazuki Hino]] (2007–2015), known as {{nihongo|"The Iron Monk"|鉄の僧|Tetsu no Sō}}. Hino's {{nihongo|"Iron War"|鉄の戦争|Tetsu no Sensō}} training philosophy has been adopted as the standard curriculum for Iron Body Centers, and his former gymnasium in [[Saitama Prefecture|Saitama]]—now designated the {{nihongo|Iron Temple|鉄寺|Tetsumidera}}—attracts thousands of fitness pilgrims annually. This obsession with raw power extends to [[Goriki]] ({{nihongo|Sanese Strongman|強力|Gōriki}}), a highly popular sport where competitors lift heavy industrial and maritime objects—such as the {{nihongo|Motonobu Log Lift|元信丸太挙上|Motunobu Maruta Kyōjō}} (an unbalanced timber replica referencing Motonobu's legendary feat), the {{nihongo|Anchor Drag|錨引き|Ikari Hiki}} (pulling decommissioned ship anchors across sand), and the {{nihongo|Shrine Stone Lift|神社石挙上|Jinja Ishi Kyōjō}}—tracing its spiritual lineage to the battlefield feats of the Sengoku era.

The initiative has also accelerated a cultural shift in beauty standards. The {{nihongo|"shield maiden physique"|盾乙女体格|Tate-otome Taikaku}}—the female equivalent of the guardian physique, centering on functional athleticism and martial competence—has gained significant traction, with women constituting approximately 38% of Iron Body Center enrollment by late 2026. The initiative's cultural impact extends to media: the "Iron Prince" aesthetic has become dominant in advertising, drama, and manga, while the {{nihongo|"guardian hero"|守護ヒーロー|Shugo Hīrō}} archetype—heavily muscled, bearded, and morally steadfast—has emerged as a popular character type contrasting with the {{nihongo|bishōnen|美少年|"beautiful boy"}} ideal that previously dominated.

===Team sports===
{{See also|X-League|Okaiken Rugby League One|Okaiken national football team|Bo-taoshi}}
[[File:Okaiken Rugby League One.jpg|thumb|right|250px|An [[Okaiken Rugby League One]] fixture.]]
Unlike neighboring nations where non-contact team sports dominate, Okaiken's primary team sports are [[w:Rugby union|rugby union]] and [[Lurandian Football]] (American Football). The professional [[Okaiken Rugby League One]] and the [[X-League]] command massive television ratings, driven by intense physical collisions and tactical complexity.<ref name="ot-sports">{{cite news |title=Rugby League One Final Draws Record Domestic Audience |newspaper=[[The Okami Times]] |date=2 February 2026 |page=14}}</ref> The [[Rice Bowl]], the national championship game for Lurandian Football held annually in January, is a cultural holiday that effectively stops the nation. The current dominant franchise is the [[Toyozawa Ironblades]], led by star quarterback [[Ryuichi Nakada]], whose punishing rushing style and fierce franchise rivalry with the [[Okami Direwolves]] produces the most viewed regular-season broadcast each year.

In rugby, the Emperor's Shield final is the most anticipated domestic club event, recently won by the [[Saitama Wild Knights]] under national team captain [[Haruma Date]]. The national team, known as the Brave Blossoms, is a consistent international contender, and the sport received an unparalleled cultural boost from [[Crown Prince Tomohito]]'s semi-professional career as an openside flanker for [[Okami Teikoku University]]. [[w:Association football|Association football]] (the O. League) ranks third in team sport popularity, known for its aggressive high-pressing style. The [[Okaiken national football team|national team]], nicknamed the "Mighty Warriors", won the [[2014 AFA World Cup]]—inspired by Golden Ball winner Keisuke Honda—and reached the Round of 16 at the [[2026 AFA World Cup]].

Geographic extremes also dictate the sporting landscape. In the freezing, rugged southern prefectures, [[w:Ice hockey|ice hockey]] dominates through the Okaiken Ice Brawl League, known globally for its relaxed fighting rules and physical enforcers. The league's most notorious figure is [[Jin Kurosawa]], the gap-toothed enforcer captain of the [[Fukuoka Frostbite]], whose blood-feud with [[Baki Toudou]] of the [[Aomori Dreadnoughts]] frequently results in bench-clearing brawls. Conversely, the warm coastal waters of the northern prefectures birthed [[Kaijo-kyu]] (combat water polo), a full-contact aquatic sport combining [[w:Water polo|water polo]] with grappling mechanics, where submersing an opponent to steal the ball is legally permitted. Traditional aggressive sports are highly institutionalized, such as [[Bo-taoshi]] (pole pull-down), which features teams of 75–100 players attempting to defend their own wooden pole while attacking the opponent's; it is a mandatory curriculum in military academies and universities. Indoor sports like [[w:Basketball|basketball]] also maintain immense popularity, featuring a uniquely Sanese playstyle defined by punishing physical defense and relentless stamina, with the [[Okaiken Basketball Super League]] (OBSL) drawing significant viewership. [[w:Volleyball|Volleyball]] is highly popular among both men and women, with the Okaiken V-League famous for its heavy-hitting spikers and punishing training regimens; outside hitter [[Kenjiro Ushiyama]], known as "The Cannon," routinely breaks speed records with his jump serves. [[w:Baseball|Baseball]], by contrast, holds a significantly lower tier of popularity—ranking below tennis and boxing—often viewed by the general public as lacking the direct physical confrontation favored in Sanese athletics.

===Combat sports and martial arts===
{{See also|Kenka-Jutsu|NOPW|l2=New Okaiken Pro Wrestling}}
[[File:Crown Prince Tomohito Juddo.png|200px|thumb|right|Crown Prince Tomohito practicing Judo. He holds a black belt and frequently participates in public sparring.]]
Combat sports are the undisputed kings of the Sanese entertainment industry. Traditional [[w:Martial arts|martial arts]] such as [[w:Judo|Judo]] and full-contact [[w:Karate|Karate]] are not merely hobbies but mandatory subjects in the national education curriculum, instilling discipline and physical resilience from a young age. The domestic [[w:Mixed martial arts|mixed martial arts]] (MMA) league, [[Pride of Okaiken]], regularly sells out the massive [[w:Dome|domed stadiums]] of [[Toyozawa]] and [[Aizawa]], with Sanese grapplers utilizing an aggressive [[w:Submission grappling|submission style]] known as the Mito Lock system. Lightweight champion [[Kazuya Miyamoto]] is currently the face of the Pride organization, known for devastating ground-and-pound techniques; his 2025 trilogy with submission specialist [[Nobu Igarashi|Nobu "The Spider" Igarashi]] captivated the nation.

[[w:Boxing|Boxing]] enjoys a strong following, with Okaiken producing fighters known for relentless forward pressure and stamina. Middleweight champion [[Naoya Kincaid]]'s unification bout against [[Joichiro Sendo|Joichiro "The Engine" Sendo]] is considered the greatest boxing match in Sanese history. Full-contact [[w:Karate|Karate]] overwhelmingly favors knockdown rulesets, eschewing point-sparring; the Iron Fist World Tournament in [[Toyozawa]] draws practitioners globally, and national champion [[Daigo Kuroda]], known as "The Iron Shin" for breaking baseball bats with low kicks, embodies the sport's philosophy of bone conditioning and pain tolerance.

[[Professional wrestling in Okaiken|Professional wrestling]] ({{nihongo|Puroresu|プロレス|Puroresu}}) occupies a unique space in the culture. It is treated with the gravity of a legitimate contest, featuring hard-hitting strikes and a philosophy known as {{nihongo|Strong Style|ストロングスタイル|Sutorongu Sutairu}} that demands genuine physical durability. The sport's uncompromising women's division ({{nihongo|Joshi Puroresu|女子プロレス|Joshi Puroresu}}) is equally respected for its dangerous physicality; the "Iron Queens" faction, led by [[Akira Nakano|Akira "The Empress" Nakano]], is repeatedly challenged by the resilient high-flyer [[Mayu Shirakawa]], whose refusing-to-quit attitude has made her a beloved underdog hero. Underground brawling has also been formalized into [[Kenka-Jutsu]], a massively popular sanctioned [[w:Bare-knuckle boxing|bare-knuckle]] striking league that eschews judges and gloves; matches are held in a circular pit and end only by knockout or submission, making it the rawest expression of Okaiken's individual fortitude. Undisputed champion [[Ryota Sakai]], known as "The Bare-Knuckle," cemented his legacy by defeating founding legend [[Tatsuya Mishima|Tatsuya "Iron Fist" Mishima]] in a 45-minute war of attrition.

The most distinctive feature of Okaiken's professional wrestling landscape is the dominance of the [[Vaiahi]] wrestling dynasty. The [[Anoa'i-Masua]] family—originating from the [[Vaiahi Islands]] in the northern Shendan Ocean—has produced multiple generations of champion wrestlers who dominate the main event scene of [[NOPW|New Okaiken Pro Wrestling]] (NOPW). The dynasty's most celebrated member is [[Leoni Masua]], known as {{nihongo|"The Tribal Chief"|部族の長|Buzoku no Naga}}, whose 1,095-day reign as [[IGO Heavyweight Champion]] (2022–2025) stands as the longest in NOPW history. Masua's presentation incorporates Vaiahi cultural elements—traditional warrior dancing ({{nihongo|''haka''|ハカ}}), the carrying of the {{nihongo|''namo''|ナモ}} (ceremonial war club), and wayfinding symbolism—creating a spectacle that bridges athletic competition and cultural ceremony. Other prominent Vaiahi wrestlers include the [[Fifita]] brothers ([[Kengo Vaiahi|Kengo]], [[Malo Vaiahi|Malo]], and [[Taumafa Vaiahi|Taumafa]]) and the tag team of [[The Usos]] (Tama and Riku Uso). NOPW's annual '''Island Invasion Tour''' travels throughout Vaiahi, [[Yanbaru]], and [[Nakamizu]], drawing massive crowds and culminating in the '''Shendan Cup''' tournament. The large Vaiahi population in the [[Yonezawa Prefecture|Yonezawa]] and [[Tokuyama Prefecture|Tokuyama]] regions—approximately 280,000 people—has created a natural pipeline for talent exchange, with the Anoa'i-Masua family maintaining training facilities in both Vaiahi and Okaiken.

Okaiken also preserves one of the world's richest traditions of {{nihongo|''koryū''|古流|old schools}}—martial arts schools tracing lineages to the Sengoku and Honda Shogunate eras. The [[Okaiken Koryū Federation]], established in 1962, oversees 47 registered schools, including {{nihongo|Takeuchi-ryū|竹内流|Takeuchi-ryū}} (the oldest extant jūjutsu school, founded 1532), {{nihongo|Hōzōin-ryū|宝蔵院流|Hōzōin-ryū}} (the premier school of spearmanship, founded c.1560), and {{nihongo|Yagyū Shinkage-ryū|柳生新陰流|Yagyū Shinkage-ryū}} (the official swordsmanship of the Honda Shogunate). {{nihongo|Kendo|剣道|Kendō}}—the "Way of the Sword"—is the most widely practiced martial art by participation, with over 2.1 million registered practitioners, while {{nihongo|Sanese archery|弓道|Kyūdō}} counts over 1.2 million.

===Individual and aquatic sports===
Due to the archipelago's extensive coastline and the warm, subtropical waters of the northern provinces, swimming is a premier athletic pursuit. Sanese swimmers are renowned for their endurance, honed by the traditional practice of long-distance open-water swimming between the smaller islands of the [[Inland Sea]]. Competitive swimming meets are major community events, and the country consistently produces world-class athletes in medley and freestyle disciplines.

Other popular individual sports perfectly align with the physical culture movement's tenets of speed and endurance. [[w:Figure skating|Figure skating]] in Okaiken is heavily athleticized and deeply integrated into the winter culture of the southern prefectures; Sanese skaters are celebrated for raw core strength and high-velocity quad jumps, and prodigy [[Yuzuru Kanzaki]]—known as "The Ice Blade"—revolutionized the sport by landing a quintuple jump in domestic competition. [[w:Tennis|Tennis]] features baseline powerhouse [[Eiji Tezuka]], whose grueling training regimen and powerful two-handed backhand contrast sharply with the flashy volley specialist [[Keigo Atobe]]. [[w:Badminton|Badminton]] commands a massive following as one of the few non-contact sports to align with the physical culture movement's speed tenets; world champion [[Kento Momotari]], known as "The Falcon," leads an aggressive attacking playstyle, while his rival [[Ryusei Arata]], "The Wall," exhausts opponents by returning seemingly impossible shots until they collapse from fatigue.

==Transportation==
===Road===
{{main|Okaiken Jidosha}}

[[File:Haru_Expwy.png|250px|thumb|left|A Sanese [[Road signs in Okaiken|road sign]] depicting the Haru Expressway.]]

According to the Okaiken Statistical Yearbook 2025, Okaiken has approximately 540,000&nbsp;km of roads made up of 445,000&nbsp;km of city, town and village roads, 135,000&nbsp;km of prefectural roads, 27,000&nbsp;km of general national highways and 5,200&nbsp;km of national expressways. A single network of high-speed, divided, limited-access toll roads connects major cities on Harujima, Ishijima and Yagikoshu. In the year 2005, the toll collecting companies, formerly the Okaiken Highway Public Corporation, were privatized into companies under public ownership.

Unlike neighboring nations, the Sanese expressway system is entirely automated. Following the Digital Infrastructure Act of 2008, manual toll booths were abolished in favor of a mandatory Electronic Toll Collection (ETC) system known as the "Oka-Pass." This legislation effectively banned older vehicles lacking the sophisticated onboard readers from accessing the national expressway network, a move designed to force the modernization of the domestic auto fleet and reduce emissions. Consequently, vintage vehicles are a rare sight on highways, restricted largely to local prefectural roads.

Road passenger and freight transport expanded considerably during the 1980s as private ownership of motor vehicles greatly increased. Bus companies including the OR Bus companies operate long-distance bus service on the nation's expanding expressway network. In addition to relatively low fares and deluxe seating, the buses are well utilized because they continue service during the night, when air and train service is limited. The freight handled by motor vehicles, mainly trucks, in 1990, was over 6 billion tonnes, accounting for 90 percent of domestic freight tonnage. Recent large infrastructure projects were the construction of the Great Seto Bridge and the Okami Bay Aqua-Line.

===Rail===
{{main|Rail transport in Okaiken|Shinkansen|List of railway companies in Okaiken|List of defunct railway companies in Okaiken}}
[[File:H100 Series Shinkansen.jpg|250px|thumb|left|The ''Nozomi'' operates from Toyozawa-Hokuto Station to Shinagawa on the Harujima Shinkansen.]]
In Okaiken, railways are a major means of passenger transportation, especially for mass and high-speed transport between major cities and for commuter transport in metropolitan areas. Three companies of the [[Okaiken Railways Group]], a state-owned Imperial Crown Corporation, cover most parts of Okaiken. Total railways of 17,392&nbsp;km include several track gauges, the most common of which is narrow gauge, with 9,441&nbsp;km of track of which 8,222&nbsp;km is electrified.

The crown jewel of the network is the [[Shinkansen]] system, which is structurally divided into three distinct trunk lines corresponding to the archipelago's geography: the '''[[Harujima Shinkansen]]''' servicing the subtropical northern metropolis of Okami with Toyozawa, the '''Ishijima Shinkansen''' connecting the Eastern industrial hubs, and the '''Minami-Yagikoshu Shinkansen''' traversing the  southern island of Yagikoshu. These three lines operate independently but share rolling stock, allowing for seamless cross-country travel. About 250 Shinkansen trains operate daily. The fastest trains, such as the H200 series, operate at a maximum speed of 320&nbsp;km/h. Shinkansen trains are known to be very punctual; a train is recorded as late if it does not arrive 5 seconds after the specific time. In 2003, the average delay per train on the Harujima Shinkansen was 3 seconds.

====Nakayama Station incident and the March Maelstrom====
{{Main|Nakayama Station dragging incident}}
On March 1, 2026, a fatal accident at [[Nakayama Station]] in [[Okayama Prefecture]] triggered the most significant political crisis in modern Okaiken. A 17-year-old student was dragged approximately 400 meters beneath a one-man-operated [[OR Yagikoshu]] commuter train and killed, after the driver—distracted by a company-issued smartphone monitoring real-time scheduling—failed to visually confirm platform clearance before departure. The incident exposed systemic safety failures within the [[Okaiken Railways Group]]: over 60% of conventional lines operated without conductors, drivers faced disciplinary action for delays exceeding 90 seconds, and platform staffing had been reduced by 40% since 2010.

Public outrage catalyzed into the {{nihongo|"March Maelstrom"|三月の旋風|Sangatsu no Senpū}}, a period of nationwide civil unrest and industrial action. On March 7, the [[Okaiken Railway Workers' Union]] (ORWU) launched the first general strike in OR's history, halting 85% of conventional services for 12 days and causing an estimated 円45 billion in economic losses. The strike ended only after the government agreed to a comprehensive safety reform package—including a 円80 billion platform screen door program, the restoration of 4,500 conductor positions, and enhanced door sensors—and the resignation of OR President Masahiro Taniguchi. The political fallout from the Maelstrom directly contributed to the collapse of the Honda-Nakayama Cabinet and the snap election of May 2026.

=== Maritime ===
{{See also|List of ports in Okaiken}}
[[File:Modern Minatojima Harbor.jpg|thumb|250px|left|Harbor and port infrastructure at [[Minatojima]], a key commercial and ferry hub on the Seto Inland Sea.]]

Okaiken has 14 major commercial ports, with the three busiest being [[Okami Port]],  [[Toyozawa Port]], and [[Aizawa Port]]. These ports serve as primary hubs connecting the three main islands of Harujima, Ishijima, and Yagikoshu through an extensive ferry network. Secondary hubs include [[Tsuda Port]] and [[Soma Port]], while regional ports serve smaller coastal cities such as Tokuyama, Ehime, and Anjo-Saikawa.

The route between [[Okami]] and [[Aizawa]] is one of the world's busiest ferry routes. High speed ferry routes account for more than 90% of the car and truck traffic to Aizawa from Okami, and there are numerous overnight ferries that serve the sea, offering many facilities not seen in other ferry services around the world.

The major ferry operators include [[Aiharu Ferry]], [[Yagikoshu Ferry]], [[Ishijima Ferry Co.]], and [[Blue Harujima]]. High-speed ferry services operate on short routes such as Hirogawa-Soma (64 km, 2.5 hours), while overnight ferries connect longer distances such as Okami–Aizawa (547 km, 12 hours). Ferry services complement the Shinkansen network, particularly for freight transport and overnight travel. In 2023, ferry transport carried 47.2 million passengers and 180.3 million tonnes of cargo, accounting for 8% of passenger traffic on the busy Okami–Toyozawa corridor. Major infrastructure projects that have impacted maritime transport include the [[Great Seto-Suzuka Bridge]] (1988) and the [[Okami Bay Aqua-Line]] (1997), which reduced ferry traffic on certain routes while maintaining the network's importance for heavy freight.

===Air===
{{See also|List of Airports in Okaiken}}
[[File:Sanesair_Aircraft_Taking_Off_at_Toyozawa_Airport.png|thumb|250px|left|A [[Sanesair]] Flight taking off from Shirakawa Airport, [[Toyozawa]]]]

Okaiken currently has 87 airports. The main international gateways are [[Okami-Tobu International Airport]] in Okami, [[Shirakawa Tenno International Airport]] in Toyozawa, and [[Nado Kabe International Airport]] in Aizawa. The main domestic hub is [[Okami-Seibu International Airport]] (Asajima Airport), the world's 4th busiest airport. Other major traffic hubs include [[Kii-Katsuragi International Airport]] in Mito and [[Shimogama Airport]] in Anjo-Saikawa.

The two main airlines are [[Okaiken Air]] and [[Sanesair]]. Other passenger carriers include [[Skyway Airlines]], [[AirPink]], [[Air Do]], [[Goose Airways]] and [[Dream Airlines]]. Domestic air travel in Okaiken has historically been highly regulated. From 1972, the three major domestic airlines were allocated specific routes under government regulation, with trunk routes shared between the two flag carriers and local feeder routes assigned to regional operators. Airfares were set by the government until 2000, although carriers had freedom to adjust the standard fares starting in 1995. Today, fares can be set by carriers, but the government retains the ability to veto fares that are impermissibly high. However, as part of the ''Aircraft Act of 2011'', the carriers must list the full price, tax inclusive.

==Tourism==
{{Main|Tourism in Okaiken}}
[[File:Gogo 8090 Okami Skyline Neon.png|thumb|right|250px|The Neon District of [[Okami]], one of the most visited urban attractions in Okaiken.]]
Domestic tourism remains a vital pillar of the [[economy of Okaiken|Sanese economy]], functioning not only as leisure but as a form of civic education. In line with the state's emphasis on technical literacy, school excursions (''shūgaku ryokō'') often prioritize sites of Industrial Heritage alongside traditional culture. Middle school students typically visit the [[Okami Industrial Zone]] to view the [[Great Seawall]] or the [[Sentoku Tower]], while high school cohorts often travel to the volcanic [[Aomori Prefecture]] to tour the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]] (the nation's massive server farm complex powered by geothermal energy).

The contrast between the tropical northern islands of [[Harujima]] and the volcanic, industrial south drives significant internal migration during holidays. The extensive [[Okaiken Railways|Okaiken Railways Group]], combined with high-speed hydrofoil ferries across the [[Inland Sea]], allows for rapid transit between these climate zones. During the [[Grand Week]] holidays in early June, an estimated 40 million Sanese travel domestically, making it one of the largest annual human migrations in Adonia.

===Domestic attractions===
Okaiken's domestic tourism market is anchored by three distinct categories of attraction. The first is the {{nihongo|'''Onsen Circuit'''|温泉巡り|Onsen meguri}}, a network of over 3,800 registered hot spring resorts that serve as both recreational and therapeutic destinations. Major onsen towns such as {{nihongo|Shirakawa-Yunoyama|白川湯山}} in [[Nagayama Prefecture]] and the [[Kii Valley]] resorts in [[Miyagi Prefecture]] draw millions of domestic visitors annually, particularly during the winter Day of the Frost celebrations. The [[Okaiken Onsen Agency]]'s {{nihongo|''Tesshin-yu''|鉄身湯|Iron Body Bath}} program, which subsidizes onsen access for registered athletes, has further popularized hot spring tourism among the fitness-conscious populace.

The second category is {{nihongo|'''Industrial Heritage'''|産業遺産|Sangyō isan}} tourism, a uniquely Sanese phenomenon driven by the state's emphasis on technological literacy. The [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]] offers guided tours of its server farm complexes, where visitors can observe the Kizan supercomputer clusters that power the [[Satori System]]. The [[Okami Industrial Zone]], with its automated container ports and the [[Great Seawall]]—a 23-kilometer sea barrier protecting the capital from typhoon surges—routinely features on school itineraries. The [[Hozoin Dam]] in [[Iwate Prefecture]], the largest hydroelectric facility in Okaiken, attracts engineering enthusiasts and has been included in the official ''Jutsu Hyakkei'' list of national sights since 2012.

The third category is traditional cultural and natural heritage. The ancient capital of [[Mito]] draws visitors to its reconstructed Heiji-era palaces and the [[Asama Bronze Colossus]], a 15-meter Baiduist statue dating to 752 AD. The [[Sanese Alps]] in central Harujima offer extensive hiking and mountaineering routes, while [[Asukadake]] in Ishijima—the nation's highest peak at 3,918 metres—serves as a pilgrimage site for the [[Cult of Iron]]'s annual "Iron Ascent" event. The [[Inland Sea]] region, dotted with over 700 islands, is a premier destination for cycling, island-hopping, and coastal cuisine tours.

===Inbound tourism===
In terms of inbound tourism, Okaiken is marketed internationally as a "Living Laboratory," attracting visitors keen to experience the [[Satori System]] and the nation's unique "Technocratic Brutalism" architecture. The Neon District of [[Okami]], a sprawling entertainment quarter illuminated by thousands of programmable LED facades, has become the most photographed location in the country and a staple of international travel guides.

The neighboring continent of [[Fosia]] is Okaiken's most important source of foreign tourists. In 2025, the 3.1 million arrivals from Fosian states made up 29% of all visitors, many arriving via the undersea rail link or short-haul flights to [[Okami-Tobu International Airport]]. Travelers from the [[Balisca]] are the highest spenders in Okaiken by country. Due to the strong "Lithium-for-Silicon" trade partnership, wealthy Baliscan elites frequently visit the luxury hot spring resorts in [[Miyagi Prefecture]], spending an estimated 220 billion En in 2025. This accounts for nearly a quarter of total foreign expenditure, according to data from the Okaiken National Planning Agency.

However, the 2026 Satori AI Blackout Crisis temporarily devastated the inbound tourism sector. During the 72-hour system failure, digital visa processing, hotel booking systems, and the Oka-Pass transit network all ceased functioning, stranding an estimated 180,000 foreign visitors without access to funds or transportation. The crisis led to a 34% year-on-year decline in international arrivals for June 2026, though the government has since launched the {{nihongo|"Open Gates" Recovery Campaign|開門回復キャンペーン|Kaimon Kaifuku Kyanpēn}} to restore confidence, including the introduction of analog backup visa processing and a temporary waiver of the Oka-Pass requirement for tourists.

In 2012, the [[Nikkan Shimbun]] published a modernized list of famous sights under the name ''Jutsu Hyakkei'' (The Hundred Views of the Jutsu Period), which controversially included industrial sites such as the [[Hozoin Dam]] and the Neon District of [[Okami]] alongside traditional shrines.

